/* Periodic Coast question bank - Gym 10 (Intermolecular forces, phases and solids;
   Silberberg 12.1-12.3 and 12.6; lessons 20-22) and Gym 11 (Solutions, colligative
   properties and biomolecules; Silberberg 13.1, 13.6 and 15.6; lessons 23-24), both
   tested on Evening Exam 3 (Nov 19) and the Final.

   Written in the style of Silberberg sample problems and end-of-chapter problem types
   (Achieve homework): identifying every IMF in a substance, ranking boiling points and
   vapor pressures, heating-curve heat sums, Clausius-Clapeyron, phase diagrams in words,
   cubic unit cells (atoms per cell, edge-radius relations, density and back-solving),
   types of solids; like-dissolves-like, solution enthalpy, Henry's law, molality and
   mole fraction, van 't Hoff factors, Raoult's law, boiling/freezing point shifts,
   osmotic pressure, molar mass from colligative data, and protein / polysaccharide /
   DNA structure and the forces that hold it.

   Every numeric answer and numeric distractor was computed with Node scripts (heating
   curves with c(ice) 2.09, c(water) 4.184, c(steam) 2.01 J/(g·°C), ΔH(fus) 6.02 and
   ΔH(vap) 40.7 kJ/mol, M(H₂O) 18.02 g/mol; R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) or 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K);
   N_A = 6.022 × 10²³; K = °C + 273.15; atomic masses from the course data sheet). */
chemAddQuestions([
/* ---------------- Gym 10 · Lesson 20: intermolecular forces ---------------- */
["m10-a-001",10,20,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "When liquid water boils at 100 °C, what is overcome?",
 ["The O−H covalent bonds inside each water molecule","Nothing is overcome; the molecules only speed up","Both the O−H bonds and the attractions between molecules","Ionic bonds between H⁺ and OH⁻ ions","Hydrogen bonds and other attractions between water molecules"],4,
 "Boiling is a physical change: the vapor is still H₂O molecules, so no covalent (intramolecular) bonds break. What gives way is the set of INTERmolecular forces - mostly hydrogen bonds, plus dispersion and dipole-dipole forces - that hold the molecules together in the liquid. Breaking O−H bonds would give H and O atoms (a chemical change, ~460 kJ per mole of bonds, far more than ΔH(vap) = 40.7 kJ/mol). Water is not made of H⁺ and OH⁻ ions, and on the boiling plateau the temperature (average kinetic energy) does not even change - the energy goes into separating the molecules.",
 ["Is steam still made of H₂O molecules?","Intra- = within a molecule; inter- = between molecules. Which one does a phase change disrupt?","Steam is H₂O, so the bonds inside the molecules survive; only the forces between molecules break."]],
["m10-a-002",10,20,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which intermolecular force is present between ALL molecules, polar or nonpolar?",
 ["Dipole-dipole forces","Hydrogen bonding","Ion-dipole forces","London dispersion forces","Ion-induced dipole forces"],3,
 "Every molecule has an electron cloud that can momentarily shift, creating an instantaneous dipole that induces a dipole in a neighbour. That attraction - the London dispersion force - acts between all particles, even He atoms and CH₄ molecules. Dipole-dipole forces need permanent dipoles (polar molecules); hydrogen bonding needs H on N, O or F; ion-dipole and ion-induced dipole forces need an ion.",
 ["Think about which force needs nothing special - no charge, no dipole, no N/O/F.","Even nonpolar He and CH₄ condense when cooled enough. What holds them together?","Instantaneous dipoles in every electron cloud: dispersion forces."]],
["m10-a-003",10,20,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "A hydrogen bond forms when a hydrogen atom covalently bonded to which atoms is attracted to a lone pair on another such atom?",
 ["N, O or F","Any atom more electronegative than carbon","C, N or O","Cl, Br or I","Any atom that has a lone pair"],0,
 "Only N, O and F are small enough and electronegative enough to leave an H atom almost bare, so the H can get very close to a lone pair on a neighbouring N, O or F. Cl is as electronegative as N but much larger, so HCl does not hydrogen bond; C−H bonds are nearly nonpolar and never hydrogen bond. Having a lone pair is needed on the ACCEPTOR, but the donor H must sit on N, O or F.",
 ["The famous three elements are small and very electronegative.","Think of the molecules that H-bond: H₂O, NH₃, HF, alcohols, amines.","N, O, F - nothing else."]],
["m10-a-004",10,20,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "London dispersion forces arise from:",
 ["the attraction between permanent dipoles of polar molecules","the attraction between an ion and a polar molecule","momentary (instantaneous) dipoles that induce dipoles in neighbouring particles","a hydrogen atom bridging two electronegative atoms","the sharing of electron pairs between neighbouring molecules"],2,
 "At any instant the electrons of an atom or molecule may be unevenly distributed, giving a fleeting dipole. That dipole distorts (polarizes) the electron cloud of a neighbour, and the two temporary dipoles attract. Permanent dipoles describe dipole-dipole forces; ion + polar molecule is ion-dipole; the H bridge is a hydrogen bond; sharing electron pairs is a covalent bond, not an intermolecular force.",
 ["These forces exist even between neutral, nonpolar atoms.","Electron clouds are always moving. What happens at one instant?","An instantaneous dipole induces a dipole next door."]],
["m10-a-005",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which of the following substances forms hydrogen bonds between its OWN molecules in the pure liquid?",
 ["CH₃F","CH₃OCH₃","HCl","CH₃OH","H₂S"],3,
 "Methanol, CH₃OH, has an O−H bond: that H (on O) can bond to a lone pair on the O of another methanol, so it hydrogen bonds with itself. CH₃F has F, but every H is on carbon, so there is no donor. Dimethyl ether CH₃OCH₃ has an O with lone pairs (an acceptor) but all its H atoms are on C - no donor. HCl and H₂S have H on Cl and S, which are too large/not electronegative enough for hydrogen bonding; they show dipole-dipole forces instead.",
 ["A molecule H-bonds with itself only if it has BOTH an H on N/O/F and a lone pair on N/O/F.","Look for an O−H, N−H or F−H bond, not just an O or F somewhere.","Only one choice has an O−H bond."]],
["m10-a-006",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which substance cannot hydrogen bond with itself, but can ACCEPT hydrogen bonds from water when dissolved?",
 ["CH₃OH","NH₃","CH₄","CH₃OCH₃","HF"],3,
 "Dimethyl ether's O has two lone pairs, so a water H can bond to it (ether = acceptor), but every H in CH₃OCH₃ is on carbon, so two ether molecules have no H to donate to each other. CH₃OH, NH₃ and HF have H on O, N or F and hydrogen bond with themselves. CH₄ has no lone pairs and no N/O/F, so it neither donates nor accepts.",
 ["Separate the two roles: donor (H on N/O/F) and acceptor (lone pair on N/O/F).","You want an acceptor that is not a donor.","An O with lone pairs but all H atoms on carbon - an ether."]],
["m10-a-007",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which intermolecular forces are present in liquid dichloromethane, CH₂Cl₂?",
 ["Dispersion forces only","Dipole-dipole forces only","Hydrogen bonding and dispersion forces","Dispersion, dipole-dipole and hydrogen bonding","Dispersion and dipole-dipole forces"],4,
 "CH₂Cl₂ is tetrahedral with two different kinds of outer atoms, so the C−Cl bond dipoles do not cancel: the molecule is polar and has dipole-dipole forces. Like every molecule it also has dispersion forces (here quite large, because Cl atoms are polarizable). There is no hydrogen bonding: the H atoms are bonded to C, not N, O or F. 'Dipole-dipole only' forgets that dispersion is always there.",
 ["List the IMFs one at a time: dispersion (always), dipole-dipole (if polar), H-bond (if H on N/O/F).","Is CH₂Cl₂ polar? Its two H and two Cl around C do not cancel.","Polar, no N−H/O−H/F−H: dispersion plus dipole-dipole."]],
["m10-a-008",10,20,2,"Trap","mcq",
 "Carbon tetrachloride, CCl₄, has four polar C−Cl bonds. Which intermolecular forces act between CCl₄ molecules?",
 ["Dispersion forces only","Dipole-dipole forces only","Dispersion and dipole-dipole forces","Dipole-dipole forces and hydrogen bonding","Ion-dipole forces"],0,
 "Polar bonds are not enough: in tetrahedral CCl₄ the four identical C−Cl dipoles cancel, so the molecule has no net dipole and no dipole-dipole forces. Only London dispersion forces remain - but they are strong (CCl₄ is a liquid boiling at 77 °C) because the 74-electron molecule is very polarizable. The trap is to judge polarity bond-by-bond instead of molecule-by-molecule.",
 ["Bond polarity and molecular polarity are different questions.","What is the shape of CCl₄, and do four identical dipoles in that shape cancel?","Symmetric tetrahedron, net dipole zero: dispersion only."]],
["m10-a-009",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which intermolecular forces act between methylamine molecules, CH₃NH₂?",
 ["Dispersion only","Dispersion and dipole-dipole only","Hydrogen bonding only","Dispersion, dipole-dipole and hydrogen bonding","Ion-dipole and hydrogen bonding"],3,
 "CH₃NH₂ has N−H bonds (donors) and a lone pair on N (acceptor), so it hydrogen bonds with itself. It is polar (the N lone pair and N−H bonds give a net dipole), so dipole-dipole forces are present too, and dispersion forces act between all molecules. Hydrogen bonding is itself a special, strong dipole-dipole attraction; listing it 'only' ignores the other two. There are no ions, so no ion-dipole.",
 ["Go through the checklist: dispersion, dipole-dipole, hydrogen bonding.","Does CH₃NH₂ have an H on N, O or F?","N−H bonds plus a polar molecule: all three."]],
["m10-a-010",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which intermolecular force is mainly responsible for dissolving KCl in water?",
 ["Dispersion forces","Hydrogen bonding","Dipole-dipole forces","Ion-induced dipole forces","Ion-dipole forces"],4,
 "K⁺ and Cl⁻ ions are each surrounded by oriented water molecules: the δ− O end points toward K⁺, the δ+ H ends point toward Cl⁻. That ion-dipole attraction (hydration) is what pays for pulling the ions out of the lattice. Water-water hydrogen bonds must partly break, but they are not what holds the ions in solution. Ion-induced dipole would describe an ion near a NONpolar molecule.",
 ["What two kinds of particles meet when a salt dissolves in water?","An ion and a polar molecule.","Ion + permanent dipole = ion-dipole."]],
["m10-a-011",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "O₂ dissolves slightly in water. What is the main attractive force between a dissolved O₂ molecule and the surrounding water molecules?",
 ["Dipole-induced dipole","Hydrogen bonding","Ion-dipole","Dipole-dipole","Ion-induced dipole"],0,
 "Water is polar; O₂ is nonpolar. The permanent dipole of a water molecule distorts the electron cloud of O₂, inducing a temporary dipole, and the two attract: a dipole-induced dipole force (dispersion acts too). O₂ has no permanent dipole, so it cannot take part in dipole-dipole forces, and it has no H to hydrogen bond. No ions are involved. This weak attraction is why O₂ is only about 10⁻³ M soluble at 1 atm.",
 ["One partner is polar, the other is not.","A permanent dipole can distort a nonpolar electron cloud.","Water's dipole induces a dipole in O₂."]],
["m10-a-012",10,20,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which atom is the most polarizable?",
 ["He","Ne","Ar","Kr","Xe"],4,
 "Polarizability measures how easily an electron cloud is distorted. It increases with size: bigger atoms hold their outer electrons farther from the nucleus and more loosely. Down Group 18, Xe is the largest, so it is the most polarizable and has the strongest dispersion forces (boiling point −108 °C versus −269 °C for He).",
 ["Polarizability grows with the size of the electron cloud.","Which way does atomic size change down a group?","The heaviest, largest noble gas in the list."]],
["m10-a-013",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Arrange the noble gases He, Ne, Ar and Kr in order of INCREASING boiling point.",
 ["Kr < Ar < Ne < He","He < Ne < Ar < Kr","He < Ar < Ne < Kr","Ne < He < Ar < Kr","All four boil at the same temperature because they have no IMFs"],1,
 "The noble gases have only dispersion forces, which grow with the number of electrons and the size of the atom (polarizability). So boiling point rises with molar mass: He (−269 °C) < Ne (−246 °C) < Ar (−186 °C) < Kr (−153 °C). The reversed order is the most common slip. They do have IMFs - dispersion - or they would never liquefy.",
 ["What is the only IMF in a noble gas?","Dispersion forces grow with size and molar mass.","Lightest boils lowest."]],
["m10-a-014",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "At 25 °C and 1 atm, F₂ and Cl₂ are gases, Br₂ is a liquid and I₂ is a solid. What best explains this trend?",
 ["The X−X covalent bond gets stronger down the group","Dispersion forces increase as the molecules get larger and more polarizable","Dipole-dipole forces increase down the group","I₂ forms hydrogen bonds but F₂ does not","The heavier molecules are polar while the lighter ones are not"],1,
 "All halogens are nonpolar diatomic molecules, so dispersion is the only IMF. Down the group the molecules have more electrons in larger, more easily distorted clouds, so dispersion forces strengthen and the melting and boiling points rise until I₂ is a solid at room temperature. The X−X bond strength is an INTRAmolecular property and actually weakens from Cl₂ to I₂; none of these molecules is polar or hydrogen bonds.",
 ["Are any of these molecules polar?","Only one IMF is available. How does it depend on size?","Bigger electron cloud, stronger dispersion, higher melting and boiling points."]],
["m10-a-015",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank the following in order of INCREASING boiling point: C₃H₈, CH₄, C₄H₁₀, C₂H₆.",
 ["CH₄ < C₂H₆ < C₃H₈ < C₄H₁₀","C₄H₁₀ < C₃H₈ < C₂H₆ < CH₄","CH₄ < C₃H₈ < C₂H₆ < C₄H₁₀","C₂H₆ < CH₄ < C₃H₈ < C₄H₁₀","They all have the same boiling point because they are all nonpolar"],0,
 "These alkanes are nonpolar, so only dispersion forces act. Dispersion grows with molar mass and molecular surface area, so each extra CH₂ raises the boiling point: CH₄ (−162 °C) < C₂H₆ (−89 °C) < C₃H₈ (−42 °C) < C₄H₁₀ (−0.5 °C). Nonpolar does not mean 'no IMFs' - it means dispersion only.",
 ["All four are hydrocarbons: what IMF do they share?","Dispersion grows with molar mass and chain length.","Count the carbons."]],
["m10-a-016",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "The three isomers of C₅H₁₂ are n-pentane (an unbranched chain), 2-methylbutane (one branch) and 2,2-dimethylpropane (neopentane, nearly spherical). Which statement is correct?",
 ["All three have the same boiling point because they have the same molar mass","Neopentane has the highest boiling point because it is the most compact","n-Pentane has the highest boiling point because it hydrogen bonds","2-Methylbutane has the highest boiling point because it is the only polar isomer","n-Pentane has the highest boiling point because its long shape gives the most surface contact for dispersion forces"],4,
 "Same formula and molar mass, so the difference is SHAPE. A long, unbranched chain can lie alongside its neighbours over a large surface, maximizing dispersion forces; a compact, spherical molecule touches its neighbours over a small area. Observed boiling points: n-pentane 36 °C > 2-methylbutane 28 °C > neopentane 10 °C. None of the isomers is significantly polar and none hydrogen bonds (all H on C).",
 ["Molar mass is identical - what else affects dispersion forces?","Picture spaghetti versus marbles: which pack with more contact?","More surface contact, stronger dispersion, higher boiling point: the unbranched chain."]],
["m10-a-017",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which C₅H₁₂ isomer has the HIGHEST vapor pressure at 0 °C?",
 ["n-Pentane (unbranched)","2-Methylbutane (one branch)","2,2-Dimethylpropane (neopentane, most branched)","All three have the same vapor pressure","It cannot be predicted without vapor-pressure data"],2,
 "Vapor pressure is highest for the liquid with the WEAKEST IMFs, because its molecules escape most easily. Among isomers of equal molar mass, the most branched, most compact molecule (neopentane) has the smallest contact area, the weakest dispersion forces, the lowest boiling point (10 °C) and therefore the highest vapor pressure at a given temperature. Picking n-pentane confuses 'strongest IMFs' with 'highest vapor pressure'.",
 ["Vapor pressure and IMF strength move in opposite directions.","Which isomer has the weakest dispersion forces?","Most compact = least contact = weakest dispersion = highest vapor pressure."]],
["m10-a-018",10,20,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Arrange H₂S, H₂O, H₂Te and H₂Se in order of INCREASING boiling point.",
 ["H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te < H₂O","H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te","H₂Te < H₂Se < H₂S < H₂O","H₂S < H₂O < H₂Se < H₂Te","H₂O < H₂Te < H₂Se < H₂S"],0,
 "From H₂S to H₂Te the molecules get heavier and more polarizable, so dispersion forces (and boiling points) rise: H₂S (−60 °C) < H₂Se (−41 °C) < H₂Te (−2 °C). Water breaks the pattern: its O−H bonds make extensive hydrogen bonds, so despite being the lightest it boils highest (100 °C). Ordering by molar mass alone puts H₂O first - the classic trap; S, Se and Te are too large to hydrogen bond.",
 ["Which one of these can hydrogen bond?","For the three that cannot, use molar mass (dispersion).","H₂S, H₂Se, H₂Te rise with mass; H₂O jumps above them all."]],
["m10-a-019",10,20,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Arrange HF, HCl, HBr and HI in order of INCREASING boiling point.",
 ["HF < HCl < HBr < HI","HI < HBr < HCl < HF","HCl < HBr < HI < HF","HCl < HF < HBr < HI","HF < HI < HBr < HCl"],2,
 "HCl, HBr and HI all have dipole-dipole and dispersion forces; from HCl to HI the dispersion forces grow with size and win out, so boiling points rise: HCl (−85 °C) < HBr (−67 °C) < HI (−35 °C). HF hydrogen bonds (H on F), which lifts it far above the trend to 19.5 °C even though it is the lightest. Ranking by dipole size (HF > HCl > HBr > HI) or by molar mass alone gives the wrong answer.",
 ["Only one hydrogen halide can hydrogen bond.","For HCl, HBr, HI the larger molecules have stronger dispersion forces, and dispersion dominates.","Heavier halides boil higher, and HF jumps to the top."]],
["m10-a-020",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH) and dimethyl ether (CH₃OCH₃) have the same formula, C₂H₆O. Ethanol boils at 78 °C and dimethyl ether at −24 °C. Why?",
 ["Ethanol has a larger molar mass","Dimethyl ether forms hydrogen bonds that weaken its other IMFs","Dimethyl ether is nonpolar, so it has dispersion forces only","Ethanol has stronger covalent bonds that must be broken on boiling","Ethanol molecules hydrogen bond to each other; dimethyl ether molecules cannot"],4,
 "The molar masses are identical (46.07 g/mol), so dispersion forces are similar. Ethanol has an O−H bond, so its molecules hydrogen bond to each other; dimethyl ether's H atoms are all on carbon, so it has only dipole-dipole and dispersion forces. (Dimethyl ether is polar - it is bent at O - so 'nonpolar' is wrong.) Boiling never breaks covalent bonds.",
 ["Same formula means same molar mass - look elsewhere.","Where are the H atoms in each molecule?","An O−H bond in one, none in the other."]],
["m10-a-021",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which liquid has the HIGHEST vapor pressure at 25 °C?",
 ["Water, H₂O","Ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH","Diethyl ether, CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃","Glycerol, HOCH₂CH(OH)CH₂OH","Ethylene glycol, HOCH₂CH₂OH"],2,
 "Vapor pressure is highest when IMFs are weakest. Diethyl ether cannot hydrogen bond with itself (no O−H), so despite its larger molar mass its molecules escape easily - it boils at 35 °C. Water, ethanol, ethylene glycol and glycerol all hydrogen bond; glycerol, with three O−H groups, has the strongest IMFs and the lowest vapor pressure of the set.",
 ["High vapor pressure means molecules escape easily: weak IMFs.","Which liquid in the list cannot hydrogen bond to itself?","No O−H group: the ether."]],
["m10-a-022",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "As the intermolecular forces in a liquid become STRONGER, which property DECREASES?",
 ["Boiling point","Viscosity","Surface tension","Vapor pressure","Enthalpy of vaporization"],3,
 "Stronger attractions hold molecules in the liquid, so fewer escape into the vapor at a given temperature: vapor pressure goes DOWN. Everything else on the list goes UP with IMF strength - a higher boiling point, more resistance to flow (viscosity), more energy to increase the surface (surface tension) and more energy to vaporize a mole (ΔH(vap)).",
 ["Which property measures molecules escaping the liquid?","Stronger attractions make escaping harder.","Fewer molecules escape: lower vapor pressure."]],
["m10-a-023",10,20,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement about intermolecular forces is FALSE?",
 ["Dipole-dipole forces between two polar molecules are always stronger than dispersion forces between two nonpolar molecules","A hydrogen bond is a strong dipole-dipole attraction involving H bonded to N, O or F","Dispersion forces are present in every molecular substance","A nonpolar molecule can have a higher boiling point than a polar one if it is much larger","Stronger intermolecular forces give a higher enthalpy of vaporization"],0,
 "'Always' is the giveaway. For molecules of SIMILAR size, a polar molecule usually beats a nonpolar one, but a large nonpolar molecule can have far stronger dispersion forces than a small polar molecule has dipole forces: nonpolar I₂ boils at 184 °C, polar HCl at −85 °C; octane (126 °C) even out-boils water. The other four statements are true.",
 ["Look for an absolute word.","Compare a big nonpolar molecule (I₂) with a small polar one (HCl).","Size can let dispersion beat dipole-dipole, so 'always stronger' is false."]],
["m10-a-024",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which compound has the HIGHEST boiling point? (All have molar masses of about 58-60 g/mol.)",
 ["Butane, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃","Propanal, CH₃CH₂CHO","Methyl ethyl ether, CH₃OCH₂CH₃","1-Propanol, CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","Acetone, CH₃COCH₃"],3,
 "With molar mass held about equal, dispersion forces are similar, so the deciding factor is the strongest special IMF. 1-Propanol has an O−H bond and hydrogen bonds (bp 97 °C). Propanal (49 °C) and acetone (56 °C) are polar with only dipole-dipole forces (their O accepts H-bonds but there is no O−H donor); the ether (7 °C) is weakly polar; butane (−0.5 °C) is nonpolar with dispersion only.",
 ["Similar molar masses mean similar dispersion forces.","Which molecule can donate a hydrogen bond?","Only one has an O−H group: the alcohol."]],
["m10-a-025",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank propane (CH₃CH₂CH₃, 44 g/mol), dimethyl ether (CH₃OCH₃, 46 g/mol) and ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH, 46 g/mol) in order of INCREASING boiling point.",
 ["Propane < dimethyl ether < ethanol","Ethanol < dimethyl ether < propane","Dimethyl ether < propane < ethanol","Propane < ethanol < dimethyl ether","All three are about the same because their molar masses are about the same"],0,
 "Molar masses are nearly equal, so compare the other IMFs. Propane is nonpolar: dispersion only (bp −42 °C). Dimethyl ether is polar: dispersion + dipole-dipole (bp −24 °C). Ethanol adds hydrogen bonding through its O−H (bp 78 °C). Similar molar mass is exactly the situation where polarity and H-bonding decide the ranking.",
 ["Equal molar masses - so look at polarity and H-bonding.","Assign each its IMFs: dispersion only / + dipole-dipole / + H-bond.","Nonpolar < polar < hydrogen bonding."]],
["m10-a-026",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Acetone, CH₃C(=O)CH₃, is completely miscible with water. Which statement correctly describes the hydrogen bonding involved?",
 ["Acetone donates H-bonds to water through its C−H bonds","Acetone molecules hydrogen bond strongly to each other","Water's O−H hydrogen bonds to a lone pair on acetone's carbonyl oxygen; acetone is an acceptor only","Acetone and water form ionic bonds","No hydrogen bonding is possible because acetone contains no N−H or O−H bond"],2,
 "A hydrogen bond needs a donor (H on N, O or F) and an acceptor (lone pair on N, O or F). Acetone's carbonyl O has lone pairs, so it can ACCEPT an H-bond from water's O−H, but all of acetone's H atoms are on carbon, so it cannot donate. That is why pure acetone has no hydrogen bonding (dipole-dipole only) yet still hydrogen bonds to water. Saying 'no H-bonding at all' forgets that only ONE partner needs the N/O/F−H.",
 ["Split hydrogen bonding into donor and acceptor roles.","Which molecule supplies the H, and which supplies the lone pair?","Water donates; acetone's O accepts."]],
["m10-a-027",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "How many of the following can hydrogen bond with molecules of their OWN kind? CH₃OH, HF, NH₃, CH₃CHO, (CH₃)₃N, H₂O₂, CH₃F",
 ["2","3","4","5","6"],2,
 "Self H-bonding needs an H on N, O or F (donor) and a lone pair on N, O or F (acceptor). Yes: CH₃OH (O−H), HF (F−H), NH₃ (N−H), H₂O₂ (two O−H) - four. No: CH₃CHO (acetaldehyde - O is an acceptor, but every H is on C), (CH₃)₃N (trimethylamine - N has a lone pair but no N−H), CH₃F (F present, but H on C). Counting every molecule that merely contains N, O or F gives 7; that is the trap.",
 ["Check each for an H directly bonded to N, O or F.","Trimethylamine and acetaldehyde have N or O, but where are their H atoms?","CH₃OH, HF, NH₃, H₂O₂."]],
["m10-a-028",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which liquid is the most VISCOUS at 25 °C?",
 ["Hexane, C₆H₁₄","Diethyl ether, (C₂H₅)₂O","Ethanol, C₂H₅OH","Water, H₂O","Glycerol, C₃H₅(OH)₃"],4,
 "Viscosity (resistance to flow) increases with IMF strength and with molecules that tangle or link to many neighbours. Glycerol has three O−H groups per molecule, so each molecule hydrogen bonds to several neighbours at once, making it syrupy (well over 1000 times more viscous than water). Ethanol and water hydrogen bond less extensively; hexane and diethyl ether have no hydrogen bonding.",
 ["Viscosity rises with the strength and number of IMFs per molecule.","Count O−H groups per molecule.","Three O−H groups: glycerol."]],
["m10-a-029",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which liquid has the HIGHEST surface tension at 20 °C?",
 ["Pentane","Hexane","Diethyl ether","Ethanol","Water"],4,
 "Surface tension is the energy needed to increase a liquid's surface; molecules at the surface are pulled inward by their neighbours, so stronger IMFs give higher surface tension. Water, with an extensive 3-D hydrogen-bond network (each molecule two donors and two acceptors), has a very high surface tension (73 mJ/m²) - enough for insects to walk on. Ethanol hydrogen bonds less (22 mJ/m²); the hydrocarbons and ether have weaker forces still.",
 ["Surface tension tracks IMF strength.","Which liquid has the most extensive hydrogen bonding?","Two O−H donors and two lone pairs per molecule."]],
["m10-a-030",10,20,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "A substance has a definite volume but no definite shape: it takes the shape of the bottom of its container. In the kinetic-molecular picture, which describes its particles?",
 ["Far apart, moving randomly, with negligible attractions","Close together and locked in fixed positions, only vibrating","Close together and not moving at all","Far apart and locked in fixed positions","Close together but free to move past one another"],4,
 "A definite volume but variable shape is a liquid. In a liquid the particles are in contact (so the volume hardly changes), but the attractions are not strong enough to lock them in place, so they tumble past each other and the liquid flows. Fixed positions describe a solid (definite shape); far apart and fast describes a gas (no definite volume). Particles never stop moving entirely.",
 ["Definite volume, no definite shape: which phase?","Liquids are nearly incompressible but flow.","Close together yet mobile."]],
["m10-a-031",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which list puts these attractions in order from STRONGEST to WEAKEST, for typical small molecules or ions?",
 ["Covalent bond > ion-dipole > hydrogen bond > dipole-dipole > dispersion","Ion-dipole > covalent bond > dipole-dipole > hydrogen bond > dispersion","Hydrogen bond > covalent bond > ion-dipole > dispersion > dipole-dipole","Dispersion > dipole-dipole > hydrogen bond > ion-dipole > covalent bond","Covalent bond > hydrogen bond > ion-dipole > dispersion > dipole-dipole"],0,
 "Bonds (intramolecular, a few hundred kJ/mol) are far stronger than any intermolecular force. Among IMFs: ion-dipole (full charge on one partner, roughly 40-600 kJ/mol) > hydrogen bond (about 10-40 kJ/mol) > dipole-dipole (5-25 kJ/mol) > dispersion for small molecules (0.05-40 kJ/mol). The warning: dispersion grows with size, so for large molecules it can beat dipole forces.",
 ["Intramolecular bonds are always stronger than intermolecular forces.","Among IMFs, a full ionic charge beats partial charges.","Bond, then ion-dipole, H-bond, dipole-dipole, dispersion."]],
["m10-a-032",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Br₂ (159.8 g/mol) and ICl (162.4 g/mol) have almost the same molar mass and the same number of electrons. ICl boils at 97 °C and Br₂ at 59 °C. Why?",
 ["ICl has stronger dispersion forces because I is larger than Br","ICl is polar, so it has dipole-dipole forces in addition to dispersion forces","ICl hydrogen bonds","Br₂ is ionic","The I−Cl covalent bond is stronger than the Br−Br bond"],1,
 "With nearly identical molar masses and electron counts, the dispersion forces are about equal. ICl has a polar bond (Cl is more electronegative than I), so as a diatomic it has a net dipole and adds dipole-dipole forces; Br₂ is nonpolar. The extra attraction raises the boiling point. No H atoms are present, so no H-bonding; covalent bond strength does not govern boiling.",
 ["The question removed molar mass as an explanation on purpose.","Which molecule has a permanent dipole?","Two different halogens give a polar bond: dipole-dipole forces."]],
["m10-a-033",10,20,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which substance has the HIGHEST normal boiling point?",
 ["H₂O (18.02 g/mol)","HF (20.01 g/mol)","NH₃ (17.03 g/mol)","CH₃OH (32.04 g/mol)","n-Octane, C₈H₁₈ (114.2 g/mol)"],4,
 "Hydrogen bonding is not automatically the winner. n-Octane is nonpolar, but it is a long chain with 66 electrons and a large surface, so its dispersion forces exceed the total IMFs of the small H-bonding molecules: octane boils at 126 °C, water at 100 °C, CH₃OH at 65 °C, HF at 20 °C and NH₃ at −33 °C. The trap is choosing water because 'H-bonding is the strongest IMF'.",
 ["Do not assume H-bonding always wins.","Compare sizes: one of these molecules is many times heavier and longer.","A long hydrocarbon chain has very large dispersion forces."]],
["m10-a-034",10,20,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "F is more electronegative than O, yet H₂O boils at 100 °C and HF at only 19.5 °C. What is the best explanation?",
 ["Each H₂O can form up to four hydrogen bonds (two H donors, two lone-pair acceptors), while each HF has only one H to donate, so HF averages fewer hydrogen bonds per molecule","HF molecules cannot hydrogen bond","HF is nonpolar","H₂O has a much larger molar mass than HF","The O−H covalent bond is stronger than the F−H bond"],0,
 "An individual F−H···F hydrogen bond is actually stronger than O−H···O, but what matters for boiling is the TOTAL attraction per molecule. Water has two H atoms and two lone pairs, so it builds an extended 3-D network with about four H-bonds per molecule; HF has three lone pairs but only one H, so it forms zigzag chains with about two H-bonds per molecule. HF is polar and does hydrogen bond; the molar masses are similar (18 vs 20); covalent bond strength is irrelevant to boiling.",
 ["The strength of ONE hydrogen bond is not the whole story.","Count donors and acceptors per molecule for H₂O and HF.","Water: 2 H + 2 lone pairs, a 3-D network. HF: 1 H, chains only."]],
["m10-a-035",10,20,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "The normal boiling points of the Group 15 hydrides are NH₃ −33 °C, PH₃ −88 °C, AsH₃ −62 °C and SbH₃ −17 °C. Which statement best explains this pattern?",
 ["Boiling point simply increases with molar mass down the group","From PH₃ to SbH₃ dispersion forces grow with size; NH₃ is far above PH₃ because it hydrogen bonds, but SbH₃'s dispersion forces are large enough to exceed even NH₃'s total attractions","NH₃ is the only nonpolar hydride, which lowers its boiling point","PH₃, AsH₃ and SbH₃ hydrogen bond, while NH₃ does not","Boiling point decreases down the group because the molecules become less polar"],1,
 "Three effects. (1) PH₃ → AsH₃ → SbH₃: larger, more polarizable molecules, stronger dispersion, rising boiling points. (2) NH₃ has N−H bonds and hydrogen bonds, so it sits far above where its small mass would put it (−33 °C instead of roughly −110 °C). (3) Even so, heavy SbH₃'s dispersion forces exceed NH₃'s total IMFs, so SbH₃ boils highest. A pure molar-mass ranking would put NH₃ last.",
 ["Treat NH₃ separately from the other three.","For PH₃, AsH₃, SbH₃ only dispersion and weak dipole forces matter.","H-bonding lifts NH₃, but very heavy SbH₃ still wins on dispersion."]],
["m10-a-036",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which ion has the STRONGEST ion-dipole attraction to water molecules?",
 ["K⁺","Na⁺","Cs⁺","Ca²⁺","Mg²⁺"],4,
 "Ion-dipole attraction is stronger for higher charge and for smaller ions (the water dipole can get closer, Coulomb's law). The 2+ ions beat the 1+ ions, and Mg²⁺ (about 72 pm) is much smaller than Ca²⁺ (about 100 pm). So Mg²⁺ has the largest charge density and the most strongly held hydration shell (its hydration enthalpy is about −1900 kJ/mol). Cs⁺, the largest 1+ ion, is the weakest.",
 ["Ion-dipole strength depends on charge and on size.","Higher charge wins first; then smaller wins.","The smaller of the two 2+ ions."]],
["m10-a-037",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "SO₂ (64.07 g/mol) boils at −10 °C, while CO₂ (44.01 g/mol) sublimes at −78 °C. Which factor contributes to the difference besides the larger molar mass of SO₂?",
 ["SO₂ hydrogen bonds","CO₂ is ionic","CO₂ has polar bonds, so it has dipole-dipole forces that SO₂ lacks","SO₂ is bent and polar, so it has dipole-dipole forces; linear CO₂ is nonpolar","SO₂ has weaker covalent bonds"],3,
 "Shape decides polarity. CO₂ is linear (two electron groups on C), so its two C=O dipoles cancel and it has dispersion forces only. SO₂ has a lone pair on S (three electron groups), so it is bent and its S=O dipoles do not cancel; it adds dipole-dipole forces. Both molecules have polar bonds, so 'polar bonds' alone settles nothing. Neither has H, so no H-bonding.",
 ["Draw the Lewis structures and count electron groups on the central atom.","Linear versus bent: which shape cancels its bond dipoles?","Bent SO₂ is polar; linear CO₂ is not."]],
["m10-a-038",10,20,3,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which pair of molecules CANNOT form a hydrogen bond with each other?",
 ["NH₃ and H₂O","CH₃OCH₃ and H₂O","HF and CH₃OH","Acetone, CH₃COCH₃, and H₂O","CH₃OCH₃ and CH₃OCH₃"],4,
 "A hydrogen bond needs a donor H (on N, O or F) on one molecule and an acceptor lone pair (on N, O or F) on the other. Two dimethyl ether molecules both have an O acceptor, but neither has an O−H, so there is no donor: no hydrogen bond. In the other pairs water, NH₃, HF or CH₃OH supplies the donor H, and the ether or acetone O can accept it.",
 ["For each pair ask: who donates the H, and who accepts it?","An ether or ketone can accept but never donate.","Two acceptors and no donor = no hydrogen bond."]],
["m10-a-039",10,20,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which substance has dipole-dipole forces but NO hydrogen bonding in the pure liquid?",
 ["CH₃OH","NH₃","CH₃Cl","CO₂","CH₄"],2,
 "CH₃Cl is tetrahedral with one Cl and three H atoms, so the bond dipoles do not cancel: polar, with dipole-dipole forces. Its H atoms are on carbon, so it cannot hydrogen bond. CH₃OH and NH₃ hydrogen bond (O−H, N−H). CO₂ (linear) and CH₄ (symmetric tetrahedron) are nonpolar with dispersion only.",
 ["You need a polar molecule with no N−H, O−H or F−H.","Which choices are nonpolar? Which have H on N or O?","Polar, but H only on carbon."]],
["m10-a-040",10,20,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Liquid A has a higher normal boiling point than liquid B. Which statement must be true?",
 ["A has a higher vapor pressure than B at any given temperature","A must be able to hydrogen bond and B must not","A has a smaller molar mass than B","A has a lower enthalpy of vaporization than B","A has stronger intermolecular forces and a lower vapor pressure than B at the same temperature"],4,
 "A higher boiling point means A's molecules attract each other more strongly, so at any common temperature fewer of them escape: lower vapor pressure, and more energy per mole to vaporize (larger ΔH(vap)). You cannot conclude anything about molar mass or hydrogen bonding specifically - stronger IMFs can come from size, polarity or H-bonding.",
 ["Boiling point is where vapor pressure reaches 1 atm.","If A needs a higher temperature to reach 1 atm, how does its vapor pressure compare at the same temperature?","Stronger IMFs, lower vapor pressure."]],
["m10-a-041",10,20,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Rank these in order of INCREASING boiling point: CH₃CH₂OH (ethanol, 46 g/mol), CH₃CH₂CH₃ (propane, 44 g/mol), HOCH₂CH₂OH (ethylene glycol, 62 g/mol), CH₃CHO (acetaldehyde, 44 g/mol).",
 ["propane < acetaldehyde < ethanol < ethylene glycol","acetaldehyde < propane < ethanol < ethylene glycol","propane < ethanol < acetaldehyde < ethylene glycol","ethylene glycol < ethanol < acetaldehyde < propane","propane < acetaldehyde < ethylene glycol < ethanol"],0,
 "Sort by IMFs. Propane: nonpolar, dispersion only (bp −42 °C). Acetaldehyde: same molar mass but polar (C=O), so dipole-dipole is added; all its H atoms are on C, so no self H-bonding (bp 20 °C). Ethanol: one O−H, hydrogen bonds (bp 78 °C). Ethylene glycol: two O−H groups per molecule plus a larger mass, so far more hydrogen bonding (bp 197 °C). Putting acetaldehyde below propane ignores its dipole; putting glycol below ethanol ignores that two O−H groups make twice the H-bonds.",
 ["Sort into: nonpolar, polar without O−H, one O−H, two O−H.","Molar masses are close, so polarity and H-bonding decide.","More O−H groups per molecule means more hydrogen bonds."]],
["m10-a-042",10,20,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Hydrogen bonding, dipole-dipole forces and dispersion forces are all classified as:",
 ["intramolecular forces","covalent bonds","intermolecular forces","ionic bonds","metallic bonds"],2,
 "They act BETWEEN separate molecules, so they are intermolecular forces (also called van der Waals forces, with H-bonding as a special case). Intramolecular forces - covalent, ionic and metallic bonding - hold atoms together within a molecule or a lattice and are much stronger. Despite the name, a 'hydrogen bond' is not a covalent bond.",
 ["Inter = between; intra = within.","Do these forces join atoms into a molecule or attract whole molecules to each other?","Between molecules."]],
]);

/* ---------------- Gym 10 · Lesson 21: physical states and phase changes ---------------- */
chemAddQuestions([
["m10-a-043",10,21,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "The direct change of a solid into a gas, without passing through the liquid, is called:",
 ["sublimation","deposition","condensation","fusion","vaporization"],0,
 "Solid → gas is sublimation (dry ice, CO₂, at 1 atm; frost disappearing on a cold, dry day). Deposition is the reverse, gas → solid (frost forming). Fusion is melting (solid → liquid), vaporization is liquid → gas, condensation is gas → liquid.",
 ["Name the change that skips the liquid on the way UP in energy.","Dry ice 'smoking' at room temperature is the classic example.","Solid → gas = sublimation."]],
["m10-a-044",10,21,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which phase change is the reverse of sublimation?",
 ["Melting","Freezing","Condensation","Deposition","Evaporation"],3,
 "Sublimation is solid → gas, so its reverse is gas → solid, called deposition (frost forming directly from water vapor on a cold window). Condensation is gas → liquid; freezing is liquid → solid.",
 ["Reverse 'solid → gas'.","The product is a solid formed straight from vapor.","Gas → solid = deposition."]],
["m10-a-045",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which set contains ONLY exothermic phase changes?",
 ["Melting, vaporization, sublimation","Freezing, condensation, deposition","Melting, condensation, deposition","Freezing, vaporization, sublimation","Sublimation, deposition, condensation"],1,
 "Changes that bring particles CLOSER together (gas → liquid, liquid → solid, gas → solid) let IMFs form, releasing energy: condensation, freezing and deposition have ΔH < 0. Their reverses - vaporization, melting (fusion) and sublimation - must overcome IMFs and are endothermic (ΔH > 0). Every other choice mixes in at least one endothermic change.",
 ["Exothermic = energy released = attractions forming.","Which direction brings molecules closer together?","Toward the solid/liquid: freezing, condensation, deposition."]],
["m10-a-046",10,21,2,"Calculation","num",
 "For water, ΔH(fus) = 6.02 kJ/mol and ΔH(vap) = 40.7 kJ/mol. Use Hess's law to estimate ΔH(sub), the enthalpy of sublimation of ice. (Enter a number in kJ/mol.)",
 ["46.7 kJ/mol"],46.72,
 "Sublimation (solid → gas) can be written as two steps: melting (solid → liquid, ΔH(fus)) then vaporization (liquid → gas, ΔH(vap)). Because enthalpy is a state function, ΔH(sub) = ΔH(fus) + ΔH(vap) = 6.02 + 40.7 = 46.7 kJ/mol. Subtracting (40.7 − 6.02 = 34.7) is the common slip - both steps absorb heat, so they add.",
 ["Write sublimation as two phase changes in a row.","Solid → liquid → gas. Each step's ΔH is given.","Add them: 6.02 + 40.7."]],
["m10-a-047",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "For almost every substance, ΔH(vap) is much larger than ΔH(fus). Why?",
 ["Vaporizing separates the molecules completely, overcoming nearly all their attractions, while melting only loosens them so they can move past one another","Vaporization breaks covalent bonds, while melting breaks only IMFs","Liquids have weaker IMFs than solids, so vaporization needs more energy","Vaporization happens at a higher temperature, and ΔH always increases with temperature","Melting releases energy while vaporization absorbs it"],0,
 "In a liquid the molecules are still touching and attracting their neighbours; melting only lets them slide past each other, breaking a fraction of the attractions. Vaporizing pulls them essentially infinitely far apart, overcoming almost all the remaining attractions. For water, 40.7 kJ/mol versus 6.02 kJ/mol - nearly 7 times as much. Neither process breaks covalent bonds, and melting is endothermic, not exothermic.",
 ["Compare how far apart the molecules are in solid, liquid and gas.","Liquid molecules are still in contact; gas molecules are not.","Melting loosens; boiling separates completely."]],
["m10-a-048",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "On the heating curve of water, heat is added steadily while the ice is melting at 0 °C, yet the temperature does not change. Where does that energy go?",
 ["Into increasing the potential energy of the molecules by overcoming intermolecular attractions; their average kinetic energy stays the same","Into increasing the average kinetic energy of the molecules","Into breaking O−H covalent bonds","It is lost to the surroundings, since the temperature does not rise","Into raising the temperature of the ice, which is too small to measure"],0,
 "Temperature measures average KINETIC energy. On a flat segment all the heat goes into pulling molecules apart against their attractions, raising their POTENTIAL energy, so the temperature stays at the melting point until all the ice has melted. q = n·ΔH(fus) on this segment, not m·c·ΔT. No covalent bonds break, and the energy is not lost - it is stored and released again on freezing.",
 ["What does temperature measure at the molecular level?","If temperature is constant, the kinetic energy is constant. What other kind of energy can change?","Potential energy rises as the IMFs are overcome."]],
["m10-a-049",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Heat is added at a constant rate to ice at −20 °C until it becomes steam at 120 °C. Which describes the shape of the heating curve (temperature vs. heat added), in order?",
 ["Rising, rising, flat, rising, flat","Flat, rising, flat, rising, flat","Rising, flat, flat, rising, rising","Rising, flat, rising, flat, rising","Flat, rising, rising, flat, rising"],3,
 "(1) Ice warms −20 → 0 °C: rising (q = m·c(ice)·ΔT). (2) Ice melts at 0 °C: flat (q = n·ΔH(fus)). (3) Liquid warms 0 → 100 °C: rising (q = m·c(water)·ΔT). (4) Water boils at 100 °C: flat and LONG (q = n·ΔH(vap)). (5) Steam warms 100 → 120 °C: rising (q = m·c(steam)·ΔT). Every phase change is a plateau; every single-phase segment slopes.",
 ["Temperature rises only while there is a single phase.","There are two phase changes between −20 °C and 120 °C.","Rise, melt (flat), rise, boil (flat), rise."]],
["m10-a-050",10,21,2,"Calculation","num",
 "How much heat is needed to melt 36.0 g of ice at 0 °C? ΔH(fus) of water = 6.02 kJ/mol. (Enter a number in kJ.)",
 ["12.0 kJ"],12.03,
 "On a plateau q = n·ΔH. n = 36.0 g ÷ 18.02 g/mol = 1.998 mol. q = 1.998 mol × 6.02 kJ/mol = 12.0 kJ. There is no ΔT: the temperature stays at 0 °C, so m·c·ΔT does not apply. Multiplying 36.0 g by 6.02 kJ/mol without converting to moles (217 kJ) is the usual unit slip.",
 ["The temperature does not change while ice melts, so which formula applies?","q = n × ΔH(fus). First convert grams to moles.","36.0 ÷ 18.02 = 2.00 mol; × 6.02 kJ/mol."]],
["m10-a-051",10,21,2,"Calculation","num",
 "How many grams of ice at 0 °C can be melted by 10.0 kJ of heat? ΔH(fus) of water = 6.02 kJ/mol. (Enter a number in g.)",
 ["29.9 g"],29.93,
 "n = q ÷ ΔH(fus) = 10.0 kJ ÷ 6.02 kJ/mol = 1.661 mol. Mass = 1.661 mol × 18.02 g/mol = 29.9 g. Using ΔH(vap) (40.7 kJ/mol) instead would give 4.43 g - the amount of water 10.0 kJ could BOIL away, about seven times less.",
 ["Melting at 0 °C: q = n·ΔH(fus). Solve for n.","n = 10.0 / 6.02 mol.","Then multiply by 18.02 g/mol."]],
["m10-a-052",10,21,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "How much heat is needed to vaporize 36.0 g of liquid water at 100 °C? ΔH(vap) = 40.7 kJ/mol.",
 ["12.0 kJ","15.1 kJ","1.47 × 10³ kJ","81.3 kJ","2.26 kJ"],3,
 "n = 36.0 g ÷ 18.02 g/mol = 1.998 mol; q = 1.998 × 40.7 kJ/mol = 81.3 kJ. Distractors: 12.0 kJ uses ΔH(fus); 1.47 × 10³ kJ multiplies grams by kJ/mol (no mole conversion); 15.1 kJ is m·c·ΔT for heating the water from 0 to 100 °C, not boiling it; 2.26 kJ is the heat per GRAM (40.7 ÷ 18.02), not for 36.0 g.",
 ["Boiling at 100 °C is a plateau: q = n·ΔH(vap).","Convert 36.0 g to moles first.","2.00 mol × 40.7 kJ/mol."]],
["m10-a-053",10,21,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "How much heat is needed to convert 25.0 g of ice at −20.0 °C into liquid water at 30.0 °C? c(ice) = 2.09 J/(g·°C), c(water) = 4.184 J/(g·°C), ΔH(fus) = 6.02 kJ/mol.",
 ["4.18 kJ","8.35 kJ","13.6 kJ","12.5 kJ","3.14 kJ"],3,
 "Three segments. (1) Warm ice −20.0 → 0 °C: 25.0 × 2.09 × 20.0 = 1045 J. (2) Melt: (25.0/18.02) mol × 6020 J/mol = 8352 J. (3) Warm water 0 → 30.0 °C: 25.0 × 4.184 × 30.0 = 3138 J. Total = 12 535 J = 12.5 kJ. Distractors: 4.18 kJ skips melting; 8.35 kJ is melting only; 13.6 kJ uses c(water) for the ice; 3.14 kJ is only the last segment.",
 ["Break the path at every phase change: how many segments?","Sloped segments use m·c·ΔT with that phase's c; the plateau uses n·ΔH(fus). Keep everything in joules.","1045 J + 8352 J + 3138 J."]],
["m10-a-054",10,21,4,"Exam","num",
 "How much heat is required to convert 50.0 g of ice at −10.0 °C into steam at 110.0 °C? c(ice) = 2.09 J/(g·°C), c(water) = 4.184 J/(g·°C), c(steam) = 2.01 J/(g·°C), ΔH(fus) = 6.02 kJ/mol, ΔH(vap) = 40.7 kJ/mol. (Enter a number in kJ.)",
 ["153 kJ"],152.6,
 "n = 50.0/18.02 = 2.775 mol. (1) Ice −10.0 → 0: 50.0 × 2.09 × 10.0 = 1045 J. (2) Melt: 2.775 × 6020 = 16 704 J. (3) Water 0 → 100: 50.0 × 4.184 × 100.0 = 20 920 J. (4) Boil: 2.775 × 40 700 = 112 930 J. (5) Steam 100 → 110: 50.0 × 2.01 × 10.0 = 1005 J. Total = 152 604 J = 153 kJ. Traps: mixing kJ and J (adding 6.02 and 40.7 to joule values), skipping a plateau (no boiling gives 39.7 kJ), or using one c for every phase.",
 ["Five segments: warm, melt, warm, boil, warm.","Convert ΔH values to J/mol and grams to moles for the two plateaus; use each phase's own c on the slopes.","1045 + 16 704 + 20 920 + 112 930 + 1005 J."]],
["m10-a-055",10,21,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "In converting 50.0 g of ice at −10 °C to steam at 110 °C (total about 153 kJ), which single step absorbs the MOST heat?",
 ["Warming the ice from −10 °C to 0 °C","Melting the ice at 0 °C","Warming the liquid water from 0 °C to 100 °C","Vaporizing the water at 100 °C","Warming the steam from 100 °C to 110 °C"],3,
 "Vaporization: 2.775 mol × 40.7 kJ/mol = 112.9 kJ, about 74% of the total. Warming the liquid through 100 °C takes 20.9 kJ, melting 16.7 kJ, and the two 10-degree warmings only about 1 kJ each. Boiling must separate the molecules completely, so it dominates every heating curve of water; on a time-vs-temperature plot it is the longest plateau.",
 ["Compare ΔH(vap) with ΔH(fus) first.","40.7 kJ/mol is almost 7 times 6.02 kJ/mol.","The boiling plateau."]],
["m10-a-056",10,21,2,"Calculation","mcq",
 "How much heat is needed to warm 50.0 g of liquid water from 0.0 °C to 100.0 °C? c(water) = 4.184 J/(g·°C).",
 ["20.9 kJ","16.7 kJ","113 kJ","1.05 kJ","41.8 kJ"],0,
 "Single phase, so q = m·c·ΔT = 50.0 g × 4.184 J/(g·°C) × 100.0 °C = 20 920 J = 20.9 kJ. 16.7 kJ is the melting step and 113 kJ the boiling step for the same 50.0 g; 1.05 kJ is the 10-degree warming of ice; 41.8 kJ uses 100 g of water.",
 ["Is there a phase change between 0 °C and 100 °C (exclusive)?","Use q = m·c·ΔT.","50.0 × 4.184 × 100.0 J."]],
["m10-a-057",10,21,4,"Calculation","mcq",
 "What is q for converting 10.0 g of steam at 120.0 °C into liquid water at 60.0 °C? c(steam) = 2.01 J/(g·°C), c(water) = 4.184 J/(g·°C), ΔH(vap) = 40.7 kJ/mol.",
 ["−2.08 kJ","−22.6 kJ","+24.7 kJ","−24.7 kJ","−409 kJ"],3,
 "Heat is RELEASED at every step, so q is negative. (1) Cool steam 120 → 100 °C: 10.0 × 2.01 × 20.0 = 402 J. (2) Condense: (10.0/18.02) mol × 40 700 J/mol = 22 586 J. (3) Cool water 100 → 60 °C: 10.0 × 4.184 × 40.0 = 1674 J. Total released = 24 662 J, so q = −24.7 kJ. Distractors: +24.7 kJ has the wrong sign; −2.08 kJ leaves out condensation; −22.6 kJ is condensation alone; −409 kJ multiplies 10.0 g by 40.7 kJ/mol without converting to moles (407 kJ, plus the 2.1 kJ of cooling).",
 ["Cooling and condensing release heat: what sign must q have?","Three segments: cool the steam, condense at 100 °C, cool the liquid.","402 J + 22 586 J + 1674 J, with a minus sign."]],
["m10-a-058",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "A burn from 100 °C steam is usually much worse than a burn from the same mass of 100 °C liquid water. Why?",
 ["Steam has a higher temperature than boiling water","Steam molecules move more slowly and stick to skin","Steam is more dense than liquid water","Steam contains broken O−H bonds that react with skin","Steam first condenses on the skin, releasing ΔH(vap), and only then cools as liquid water"],4,
 "Both start at 100 °C, so they deliver the same heat while cooling as liquid. Steam, however, must first CONDENSE, releasing 40.7 kJ/mol (2.26 kJ per gram) into the skin. For 5.00 g cooling to 37 °C: condensing gives 11.3 kJ plus 1.3 kJ of cooling = 12.6 kJ for steam, versus only 1.3 kJ for the hot water.",
 ["Both are at 100 °C, so the temperature is not the difference.","What phase change happens when steam touches cooler skin?","Condensation is exothermic by ΔH(vap)."]],
["m10-a-059",10,21,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "The NORMAL boiling point of a liquid is the temperature at which:",
 ["its vapor pressure equals 1 atm (760 torr)","its vapor pressure equals the external pressure, whatever that is","it first begins to evaporate","its vapor pressure is zero","its liquid and solid phases are in equilibrium"],0,
 "A liquid boils at whatever temperature its vapor pressure equals the external pressure; the NORMAL boiling point is the special case where that external pressure is exactly 1 atm. (The second choice defines 'a' boiling point, not the normal one.) Evaporation happens at every temperature; solid-liquid equilibrium is the melting point.",
 ["'Normal' fixes the external pressure at a standard value.","Which pressure counts as standard here?","Vapor pressure = 1 atm."]],
["m10-a-060",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Water boils at about 94 °C in a mountain town. Why?",
 ["The water there has fewer hydrogen bonds","The atmospheric pressure is lower, so water's vapor pressure reaches the external pressure at a lower temperature","The air is colder, which lowers the boiling point","ΔH(vap) of water is smaller at high altitude","Gravity is weaker, so the molecules escape more easily"],1,
 "Boiling occurs when the vapor pressure equals the external pressure. At high altitude the air pressure is lower (about 0.80 atm at 2000 m), and water's vapor pressure reaches that value at about 94 °C. The IMFs and ΔH(vap) are unchanged. Foods cook more slowly because the boiling water is cooler.",
 ["What condition defines boiling?","How does the external pressure change with altitude?","Lower external pressure is matched at a lower temperature."]],
["m10-a-061",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "A pressure cooker cooks food faster than an open pot. Why?",
 ["Steam inside is denser, so it transfers more heat at 100 °C","Pressure lowers the specific heat of water","Pressure breaks the hydrogen bonds in water","The higher pressure lowers the boiling point, so water boils sooner","The higher pressure raises the boiling point, so the water inside stays liquid well above 100 °C"],4,
 "Sealing the pot raises the pressure above the water (to about 2 atm). The water does not boil until its vapor pressure matches that higher pressure, at about 120 °C, so food cooks in hotter water. Higher external pressure means a HIGHER boiling point, not a lower one.",
 ["Boiling point depends on the external pressure.","More pressure on the liquid means its vapor pressure must climb higher before it can boil.","Higher pressure, higher boiling temperature."]],
["m10-a-062",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which change INCREASES the equilibrium vapor pressure of a pure liquid in a closed container?",
 ["Adding more of the same liquid","Using a container with a larger volume","Raising the temperature","Spreading the liquid out to increase its surface area","Lowering the temperature"],2,
 "Equilibrium vapor pressure depends only on the identity of the liquid (its IMFs) and the temperature. Raising T gives more molecules enough kinetic energy to escape, so the vapor pressure rises (steeply - roughly exponentially). The amount of liquid, the container volume and the surface area change how fast equilibrium is reached, but not the final vapor pressure.",
 ["Vapor pressure is a property of the liquid at a given temperature.","Surface area changes the RATE of evaporation and condensation equally.","Only temperature (for a given liquid) changes it."]],
["m10-a-063",10,21,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "A sealed flask holds liquid water in equilibrium with its vapor at 25 °C (vapor pressure 23.8 torr). The flask volume is suddenly doubled at constant temperature, and some liquid still remains. Once equilibrium is re-established, the pressure of water vapor is:",
 ["11.9 torr, because doubling the volume halves the pressure (Boyle's law)","47.6 torr","0 torr, because all the vapor condenses","somewhere between 11.9 and 23.8 torr and depends on how much liquid is present","23.8 torr, the same as before"],4,
 "Right after the expansion the vapor pressure does drop to about 11.9 torr, but evaporation is now faster than condensation, so more liquid evaporates until the vapor pressure climbs back to the equilibrium value for 25 °C: 23.8 torr. As long as some liquid remains, the vapor pressure is fixed by temperature alone. Applying Boyle's law to a vapor in contact with its liquid is the trap.",
 ["Is vapor pressure a property of the container or of the liquid at that temperature?","After the expansion, which is faster - evaporation or condensation?","More liquid evaporates until the same equilibrium pressure is restored."]],
["m10-a-064",10,21,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "When a liquid in a closed container has reached its equilibrium vapor pressure:",
 ["the rate of evaporation equals the rate of condensation","evaporation has stopped","all of the liquid has evaporated","the rate of condensation is greater than the rate of evaporation","the temperature of the liquid begins to rise"],0,
 "This is a DYNAMIC equilibrium: molecules keep leaving the liquid and keep returning, but at equal rates, so the amount of vapor (and its pressure) stays constant. Nothing has stopped; the two opposing processes simply balance.",
 ["Equilibrium in chemistry is dynamic, not static.","Molecules still escape the surface - and still return.","Two rates are equal."]],
["m10-a-065",10,21,3,"Calculation","num",
 "Water's normal boiling point is 100.0 °C and its ΔH(vap) is 40.7 kJ/mol. Use the Clausius-Clapeyron equation to estimate the vapor pressure of water at 80.0 °C. (Enter a number in torr.)",
 ["362 torr"],361.5,
 "ln(P₂/P₁) = −(ΔH(vap)/R)(1/T₂ − 1/T₁). P₁ = 760 torr at T₁ = 373.15 K; T₂ = 353.15 K. 1/T₂ − 1/T₁ = 0.0028317 − 0.0026799 = 1.5177 × 10⁻⁴ K⁻¹. −(40 700/8.314) × 1.5177 × 10⁻⁴ = −0.7430. P₂ = 760 × e^(−0.7430) = 760 × 0.4757 = 362 torr (measured: 355 torr). Traps: using °C (nonsense), ΔH in kJ with R in J (gives about 759 torr), or a sign error (gives about 1600 torr - more than 1 atm below the boiling point, impossible).",
 ["Use T in kelvin and ΔH in J/mol with R = 8.314 J/(mol·K).","The normal boiling point gives you the pair (760 torr, 373.15 K).","P₂ = 760 × exp[−(40 700/8.314)(1/353.15 − 1/373.15)]."]],
["m10-a-066",10,21,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "At a mountain campsite the atmospheric pressure is 0.800 atm. At what temperature does water boil there? ΔH(vap) = 40.7 kJ/mol; normal boiling point 100.0 °C.",
 ["80.0 °C","93.8 °C","99.9 °C","106.5 °C","366.9 °C"],1,
 "Boiling happens when vapor pressure = external pressure, so find T₂ where P = 0.800 atm. 1/T₂ = 1/T₁ − (R/ΔH)·ln(P₂/P₁) = 1/373.15 − (8.314/40 700)·ln(0.800) = 0.0026799 + 4.558 × 10⁻⁵ = 0.0027255 K⁻¹. T₂ = 366.9 K = 93.8 °C. Distractors: 80.0 °C scales 100 °C by 0.800 (temperature is not proportional to pressure); 99.9 °C uses R = 0.08206 with ΔH in J; 106.5 °C has the sign flipped (a lower pressure must lower the boiling point); 366.9 °C forgets to convert back from kelvin.",
 ["Boiling point = temperature where vapor pressure equals the external pressure.","Solve Clausius-Clapeyron for 1/T₂ with P₁ = 1.000 atm at 373.15 K.","1/T₂ = 1/373.15 − (8.314/40 700) ln(0.800); then convert K to °C."]],
["m10-a-067",10,21,4,"Calculation","num",
 "A liquid has a vapor pressure of 100. torr at 25.0 °C and 400. torr at 60.0 °C. Calculate its enthalpy of vaporization. (Enter a number in kJ/mol.)",
 ["32.7 kJ/mol"],32.71,
 "ΔH(vap) = −R·ln(P₂/P₁) ÷ (1/T₂ − 1/T₁). T₁ = 298.15 K, T₂ = 333.15 K. ln(400/100) = 1.3863. 1/333.15 − 1/298.15 = 0.0030017 − 0.0033540 = −3.5237 × 10⁻⁴ K⁻¹. ΔH = −8.314 × 1.3863 ÷ (−3.5237 × 10⁻⁴) = 32 709 J/mol = 32.7 kJ/mol. Traps: Celsius temperatures (gives 0.494 kJ/mol), log₁₀ instead of ln (14.2 kJ/mol), or a negative answer from swapping the points in only one bracket - ΔH(vap) is always positive.",
 ["Rearrange ln(P₂/P₁) = −(ΔH/R)(1/T₂ − 1/T₁) for ΔH.","Kelvin temperatures, natural log, R = 8.314 J/(mol·K).","ΔH = −8.314 × ln 4 ÷ (1/333.15 − 1/298.15) J/mol."]],
["m10-a-068",10,21,4,"Calculation","mcq",
 "Ethanol has a vapor pressure of 115 torr at 34.9 °C and ΔH(vap) = 40.5 kJ/mol. Estimate its vapor pressure at 63.5 °C.",
 ["30.0 torr","115 torr","126 torr","441 torr","2.54 × 10³ torr"],3,
 "T₁ = 308.05 K, T₂ = 336.65 K. ln(P₂/115) = −(40 500/8.314)(1/336.65 − 1/308.05) = −(4871.3)(−2.7578 × 10⁻⁴) = 1.3434. P₂ = 115 × e^1.3434 = 115 × 3.832 = 441 torr. Distractors: 30.0 torr has the sign reversed (vapor pressure must RISE with T); 115 torr results from using ΔH in kJ with R in J (exponent nearly 0); 126 torr scales P with kelvin temperature as if it were a gas law; 2.54 × 10³ torr uses 10ˣ instead of eˣ.",
 ["Vapor pressure rises with temperature, so expect more than 115 torr.","Kelvin, J/mol, natural log.","ln(P₂/115) = −(40 500/8.314)(1/336.65 − 1/308.05)."]],
["m10-a-069",10,21,3,"Calculation","num",
 "Diethyl ether has a normal boiling point of 34.6 °C and ΔH(vap) = 26.5 kJ/mol. Estimate its vapor pressure at 20.0 °C. (Enter a number in torr.)",
 ["454 torr"],453.7,
 "Use the normal boiling point as the known point: P₁ = 760 torr at T₁ = 307.75 K. T₂ = 293.15 K. ln(P₂/760) = −(26 500/8.314)(1/293.15 − 1/307.75) = −(3187.4)(1.6183 × 10⁻⁴) = −0.5158. P₂ = 760 × e^(−0.5158) = 760 × 0.5970 = 454 torr (the measured value is about 440 torr). Ether's very high vapor pressure at room temperature is why it evaporates so quickly.",
 ["'Normal boiling point' secretly gives you a data point. What is it?","(760 torr, 307.75 K). Convert 20.0 °C to kelvin too.","P₂ = 760 × exp[−(26 500/8.314)(1/293.15 − 1/307.75)]."]],
["m10-a-070",10,21,3,"Calculation","num",
 "Inside a pressure cooker the pressure is 2.00 atm. Estimate the boiling point of water there. ΔH(vap) = 40.7 kJ/mol; normal boiling point 100.0 °C. (Enter a number in °C.)",
 ["121 °C"],120.8,
 "1/T₂ = 1/T₁ − (R/ΔH)·ln(P₂/P₁) = 1/373.15 − (8.314/40 700)·ln(2.00) = 0.0026799 − 1.4159 × 10⁻⁴ = 0.0025383 K⁻¹. T₂ = 393.97 K = 120.8 °C. Higher external pressure → higher boiling point. A sign error gives 81 °C, which cannot be right for a pressure above 1 atm.",
 ["The water boils when its vapor pressure reaches 2.00 atm.","Solve Clausius-Clapeyron for T₂ with (1.00 atm, 373.15 K) as point 1.","1/T₂ = 1/373.15 − (8.314/40 700) ln 2; convert back to °C."]],
["m10-a-071",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "A plot of ln P (vapor pressure) versus 1/T for a liquid is a straight line. What is its slope?",
 ["−ΔH(vap)/R","+ΔH(vap)/R","−R/ΔH(vap)","ΔH(vap)·R","−ΔH(vap)/T"],0,
 "The Clausius-Clapeyron equation in line form is ln P = −(ΔH(vap)/R)(1/T) + C, so y = ln P, x = 1/T, and slope = −ΔH(vap)/R. The slope is negative because vapor pressure rises as 1/T falls (T rises). A steeper line means a larger ΔH(vap) - stronger IMFs.",
 ["Write Clausius-Clapeyron as y = mx + b.","y is ln P and x is 1/T.","The coefficient of 1/T."]],
["m10-a-072",10,21,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "On a phase diagram, the triple point is:",
 ["the temperature above which a gas cannot be liquefied by pressure","the normal melting point","the point where the liquid-gas line ends","the one temperature and pressure at which solid, liquid and gas coexist in equilibrium","the temperature at which the vapor pressure is 1 atm"],3,
 "The three phase-boundary lines (solid-liquid, liquid-gas, solid-gas) meet at a single point, the triple point, where all three phases coexist. For water it is 0.01 °C and 0.006 atm; for CO₂, −56.6 °C and 5.1 atm. The end of the liquid-gas line, and the temperature above which no pressure can liquefy a gas, is the CRITICAL point.",
 ["'Triple' refers to the number of phases.","Where do the three boundary lines meet?","All three phases in equilibrium at once."]],
["m10-a-073",10,21,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Above its critical temperature and critical pressure, a substance exists as:",
 ["a solid only","a liquid that cannot be vaporized","a mixture of solid and gas","three coexisting phases","a supercritical fluid, with no distinction between liquid and gas"],4,
 "The liquid-gas line ends at the critical point. Beyond it the densities of liquid and vapor become equal and the boundary (meniscus) disappears: the substance is a single supercritical fluid. Supercritical CO₂ (above 31 °C and 73 atm) is used to decaffeinate coffee. Above the critical temperature no amount of pressure can produce a separate liquid.",
 ["What happens at the end of the liquid-gas line?","Above that point the meniscus vanishes.","One fluid phase - supercritical."]],
["m10-a-074",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Ice at −1 °C and 1 atm is compressed to a much higher pressure while the temperature is held at −1 °C. Based on water's phase diagram, what happens?",
 ["The ice sublimes","The ice melts, because water's solid-liquid line slopes to the left","Nothing; ice is stable at all pressures below 0 °C","The ice turns directly into a supercritical fluid","The ice becomes denser ice and its melting point rises"],1,
 "Water's solid-liquid line slopes LEFT (negative slope): the melting point drops as pressure rises, because liquid water is denser than ice, and pressure favours the denser phase. Moving straight up from −1 °C at 1 atm crosses that line (at roughly 130 atm), so the ice melts. For most substances the line slopes right and pressure would keep them solid.",
 ["Which phase of water is denser, ice or liquid?","Pressure favours the denser phase.","Water's solid-liquid line slopes left, so a vertical move up from just below 0 °C crosses into liquid."]],
["m10-a-075",10,21,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "CO₂ has its triple point at −56.6 °C and 5.1 atm. Why does dry ice sublime instead of melting when it warms at 1 atm?",
 ["CO₂ has no liquid phase at any pressure","Dry ice is held together by covalent bonds that break directly","CO₂ molecules are polar","Liquid CO₂ can exist only at pressures above its triple-point pressure of 5.1 atm","Its solid-liquid line slopes to the left like water's"],3,
 "On a phase diagram the liquid region begins at the triple point and extends to higher pressures. At 1 atm, below CO₂'s triple-point pressure (5.1 atm), a horizontal path from solid to higher temperature crosses only the solid-gas line, so dry ice sublimes (at −78 °C). Liquid CO₂ does exist - inside a pressurized fire extinguisher. Its solid-liquid line slopes right, the normal way.",
 ["Where on the diagram does the liquid region begin?","Compare 1 atm with the triple-point pressure.","Below the triple-point pressure, solid goes straight to gas."]],
["m10-a-076",10,21,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "A substance has its triple point at −20 °C and 0.50 atm and a normal-shaped phase diagram (solid-liquid line sloping right). A sample of the solid is held at 0.30 atm and slowly warmed. What happens?",
 ["It sublimes: at 0.30 atm, below the triple-point pressure, the solid turns directly into gas","It melts, then boils","It melts but never boils","It stays solid at all temperatures","It becomes a supercritical fluid"],0,
 "At any pressure below the triple-point pressure, the liquid region is never reached: a horizontal warming path crosses only the solid-gas line, so the solid sublimes. At pressures between the triple point and the critical point the solid would melt and then boil. This is the same reasoning as dry ice at 1 atm.",
 ["Compare the working pressure (0.30 atm) with the triple-point pressure.","Below the triple point, which boundary line does a horizontal path cross?","Only the solid-gas line: sublimation."]],
["m10-a-077",10,21,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "For most substances the solid-liquid line on a phase diagram slopes to the right (positive slope), but for water it slopes to the left. What does water's negative slope tell you?",
 ["Ice is denser than liquid water","Water has no triple point","Ice cannot exist above 1 atm","Water's melting point rises with pressure","Liquid water is denser than ice, so increasing pressure lowers the melting point"],4,
 "Pressure favours whichever phase occupies less volume. For most substances the solid is denser, so more pressure stabilizes the solid and raises the melting point (positive slope). Ice's open hydrogen-bonded lattice makes it LESS dense than liquid water (it floats), so pressure favours the liquid and lowers the melting point (negative slope). Water does have a triple point (0.01 °C, 0.006 atm).",
 ["Pressure favours the phase that takes up less volume.","Does ice float or sink in liquid water?","Liquid is denser, so pressure melts ice: melting point falls with pressure."]],
["m10-a-078",10,21,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "CO₂ has a triple point at 216.6 K and 5.1 atm and a critical point at 304.2 K and 73 atm. A sample of CO₂ gas at 320 K is compressed from 1 atm to 100 atm at constant temperature. What does it become?",
 ["Liquid CO₂, once the pressure passes 73 atm","Solid CO₂ (dry ice)","A mixture of liquid and gas separated by a meniscus","A gas that cannot be compressed further","A supercritical fluid - it never forms a separate liquid phase"],4,
 "320 K is ABOVE the critical temperature (304.2 K). Above T(c) no pressure, however large, condenses the gas into a separate liquid with a meniscus; the density simply rises smoothly. At 100 atm (also above P(c) = 73 atm) the sample is a supercritical fluid. Crossing 73 atm would produce a liquid only if the temperature were below 304.2 K.",
 ["Compare the temperature with the critical temperature first.","Above T(c), can pressure alone ever create a liquid with a meniscus?","Above both T(c) and P(c): supercritical fluid."]],
["m10-a-079",10,21,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Ice at −10 °C is heated with a burner that delivers heat at a constant rate until it is all steam. Compared with the melting plateau, the boiling plateau lasts about:",
 ["the same length of time","about 6.8 times longer","about 6.8 times shorter","about twice as long","about 100 times longer"],1,
 "At a constant heating rate, time on a plateau is proportional to the heat absorbed, n·ΔH. For the same moles of water, the ratio is ΔH(vap)/ΔH(fus) = 40.7/6.02 = 6.76, so the boiling plateau is about 6.8 times longer. This is why a pot of water takes so much longer to boil dry than an ice cube takes to melt.",
 ["Time on a plateau ∝ heat needed on that plateau.","Same moles, so compare ΔH(vap) and ΔH(fus).","40.7 ÷ 6.02."]],
["m10-a-080",10,21,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why does the evaporation of sweat cool the skin?",
 ["Sweat has a lower specific heat than skin","Evaporation releases heat into the skin","Evaporation breaks O−H bonds, which absorbs energy","The fastest (highest-energy) molecules escape, lowering the average kinetic energy of what remains; the heat of vaporization is drawn from the skin","Sweat freezes as it evaporates"],3,
 "Only molecules with enough kinetic energy to overcome the IMFs escape from the surface. Losing the most energetic molecules lowers the average kinetic energy - the temperature - of the liquid left behind, and that liquid then draws heat from the skin to keep evaporating. Vaporization is endothermic (it absorbs heat), not exothermic, and no covalent bonds break.",
 ["Is vaporization endothermic or exothermic?","Which molecules leave the liquid surface first?","The fastest escape, the average left behind drops, and the skin supplies ΔH(vap)."]],
["m10-a-081",10,21,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Liquid A has a normal boiling point of 35 °C, liquid B 78 °C and liquid C 100 °C. Rank their vapor pressures at 25 °C from HIGHEST to LOWEST.",
 ["A > B > C","C > B > A","B > A > C","A > C > B","All are equal at 25 °C"],0,
 "The lower the normal boiling point, the weaker the IMFs, and the higher the vapor pressure at any common temperature. A reaches 760 torr at only 35 °C, so at 25 °C it already has a high vapor pressure (about 500 torr for diethyl ether); C (water) has only 24 torr at 25 °C. So A > B > C. Reversing the order equates 'high boiling point' with 'high vapor pressure'.",
 ["Low boiling point means weak IMFs.","Weak IMFs mean molecules escape easily: high vapor pressure.","Lowest boiling point, highest vapor pressure."]],
["m10-a-082",10,21,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement about the phase diagram of water is FALSE?",
 ["The triple point lies at a pressure far below 1 atm","Increasing the pressure on ice just below 0 °C can melt it","At 1 atm, ice sublimes rather than melts when heated","Above the critical point, water is a supercritical fluid","The normal boiling point lies on the liquid-gas line at 1 atm"],2,
 "Water's triple-point pressure is only 0.006 atm (4.6 torr), so at 1 atm a heated sample of ice crosses the solid-liquid line (melts at 0 °C) and then the liquid-gas line (boils at 100 °C): it does not sublime. Sublimation at 1 atm is CO₂'s behaviour, because its triple point is ABOVE 1 atm. (Ice does sublime slowly at low pressures, e.g. in freeze-drying.) The other statements are true.",
 ["Compare 1 atm with water's triple-point pressure.","Sublimation on heating happens only below the triple-point pressure.","Water's triple point is at 0.006 atm, so at 1 atm ice melts."]],
]);

/* ---------------- Gym 10 · Lesson 22: the solid state ---------------- */
chemAddQuestions([
["m10-a-083",10,22,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "How many atoms belong to one face-centered cubic (FCC) unit cell?",
 ["1","2","4","8","14"],2,
 "Corner atoms are shared by 8 cells (⅛ each) and face atoms by 2 cells (½ each). FCC: 8 corners × ⅛ = 1, plus 6 faces × ½ = 3, total 4 atoms. Counting every atom drawn (8 + 6 = 14) ignores the sharing; 1 is simple cubic and 2 is body-centered cubic.",
 ["Each corner atom is shared among how many cells? Each face atom?","FCC has atoms at the 8 corners and the 6 face centers.","8 × ⅛ + 6 × ½."]],
["m10-a-084",10,22,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the coordination number (number of nearest neighbours) of an atom in a body-centered cubic (BCC) lattice?",
 ["4","6","8","12","2"],2,
 "The atom at the body center touches the 8 corner atoms of its cube, and by symmetry every atom in BCC has 8 nearest neighbours. Simple cubic is 6 (up, down, left, right, front, back); face-centered cubic and hexagonal close packing are 12. 2 is the number of atoms per BCC cell, not the coordination number.",
 ["Picture the atom at the center of the cube.","Which atoms does it touch?","All eight corners."]],
["m10-a-085",10,22,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which arrangement has the HIGHEST packing efficiency (fraction of space filled by atoms)?",
 ["Face-centered cubic (cubic close packing), 74%","Body-centered cubic, 68%","Simple cubic, 52%","Simple cubic, 74%","All cubic cells fill the same fraction of space"],0,
 "Close packing - FCC (cubic close packing) and hexagonal close packing - fills 74% of space (π/(3√2) = 0.740), the maximum for equal spheres. BCC fills 68% (√3π/8 = 0.680) and simple cubic only 52% (π/6 = 0.524). The coordination numbers follow the same order: 12 > 8 > 6.",
 ["More nearest neighbours generally means tighter packing.","Coordination numbers: SC 6, BCC 8, FCC 12.","Close packing fills 74%."]],
["m10-a-086",10,22,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "In a face-centered cubic unit cell with edge length a, the atoms touch along the face diagonal. Which relationship between a and the atomic radius r is correct?",
 ["a = 2r","a = 4r/√3","a = 2√2 r","a = 4r","a = √2 r"],2,
 "Along a face diagonal there is a corner atom (r), the face-center atom (2r) and another corner atom (r): total 4r. The face diagonal of a square of side a is √2·a, so √2·a = 4r and a = 4r/√2 = 2√2 r. a = 2r is simple cubic (atoms touch along the edge); a = 4r/√3 is BCC (atoms touch along the body diagonal, √3·a = 4r).",
 ["Where do atoms touch in FCC: the edge, the face diagonal or the body diagonal?","Face diagonal = √2·a, and it spans 4 radii.","√2·a = 4r."]],
["m10-a-087",10,22,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "In a body-centered cubic unit cell with edge length a, along which line do the atoms touch, and what is the resulting relation?",
 ["Along the edge; a = 2r","Along the body diagonal; √2·a = 4r","Along the face diagonal; √2·a = 4r","Along the body diagonal; √3·a = 4r","Along the edge; a = 4r"],3,
 "In BCC the body-center atom touches the corner atoms, so the touching line is the body (cube) diagonal: corner (r) + center (2r) + opposite corner (r) = 4r. A cube's body diagonal is √3·a, so √3·a = 4r, a = 4r/√3. Atoms along the edge of BCC do NOT touch (that is simple cubic).",
 ["Which atoms does the center atom touch?","The line from corner through center to the opposite corner is the body diagonal.","Body diagonal = √3·a = 4r."]],
["m10-a-088",10,22,3,"Calculation","num",
 "Copper crystallizes in a face-centered cubic lattice. The atomic radius of Cu is 128 pm. Calculate the density of copper. (Enter a number in g/cm³.)",
 ["8.90 g/cm³"],8.895,
 "FCC: 4 atoms per cell and a = 2√2 r = 2√2 × 128 pm = 362.0 pm = 3.620 × 10⁻⁸ cm. a³ = 4.745 × 10⁻²³ cm³. Mass of the cell = 4 × 63.55 g/mol ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 4.221 × 10⁻²² g. Density = 4.221 × 10⁻²² g ÷ 4.745 × 10⁻²³ cm³ = 8.90 g/cm³ (measured 8.96). Traps: 1 atom per cell (2.22), a = 2r (25.2), the BCC relation a = 4r/√3 (16.3), or forgetting that 1 pm = 10⁻¹⁰ cm.",
 ["You need atoms per cell, the edge length from r, and the cell volume in cm³.","FCC: 4 atoms, a = 2√2 r. Convert pm to cm (× 10⁻¹⁰).","d = (4 × 63.55 / 6.022 × 10²³) ÷ (3.620 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³."]],
["m10-a-089",10,22,4,"Calculation","mcq",
 "Iron crystallizes in a body-centered cubic lattice with an edge length of 286.6 pm. What is the density of iron?",
 ["3.94 g/cm³","7.88 g/cm³","15.8 g/cm³","1.97 g/cm³","7.88 × 10⁻⁶ g/cm³"],1,
 "BCC: 2 atoms per cell. Mass = 2 × 55.85 ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.855 × 10⁻²² g. a = 286.6 pm = 2.866 × 10⁻⁸ cm; a³ = 2.354 × 10⁻²³ cm³. Density = 1.855 × 10⁻²² ÷ 2.354 × 10⁻²³ = 7.88 g/cm³. Distractors: 3.94 uses 1 atom per cell; 15.8 uses 4 (FCC); 1.97 uses ½; 7.88 × 10⁻⁶ converts pm as if they were ångströms (1 Å = 10⁻⁸ cm) instead of 1 pm = 10⁻¹⁰ cm.",
 ["How many atoms are in a BCC cell?","Cell mass = 2 × M ÷ N_A; cell volume = a³ with a in cm.","1.855 × 10⁻²² g ÷ (2.866 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³."]],
["m10-a-090",10,22,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "Aluminum has a face-centered cubic structure with a unit-cell edge of 404.9 pm. What is the atomic radius of Al?",
 ["202 pm","175 pm","286 pm","101 pm","143 pm"],4,
 "FCC atoms touch along the face diagonal: √2·a = 4r, so r = √2·a/4 = a/(2√2) = 404.9/2.828 = 143 pm. Distractors: 202 pm is a/2 (the simple-cubic relation); 175 pm is √3·a/4 (the BCC relation); 101 pm is a/4 (forgot the √2); 286 pm is the face-diagonal half-length a/√2.",
 ["Which diagonal do FCC atoms touch along?","√2·a = 4r.","r = 404.9 ÷ (2√2)."]],
["m10-a-091",10,22,3,"Calculation","num",
 "Tungsten has a body-centered cubic structure with a unit-cell edge of 316.5 pm. Calculate the atomic radius of W. (Enter a number in pm.)",
 ["137 pm"],137.0,
 "BCC atoms touch along the body diagonal: √3·a = 4r, so r = √3 × 316.5 pm ÷ 4 = 548.2 ÷ 4 = 137 pm. Using the FCC relation gives 112 pm and a/2 gives 158 pm.",
 ["In BCC, which atoms touch?","The body diagonal √3·a spans 4 radii.","r = √3 × 316.5 / 4."]],
["m10-a-092",10,22,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Silver crystallizes in a face-centered cubic lattice and has a density of 10.5 g/cm³. What is the atomic radius of silver?",
 ["91.0 pm","144 pm","177 pm","204 pm","409 pm"],1,
 "Back-solve the edge from density. Cell mass = 4 × 107.87 ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 7.165 × 10⁻²² g. a³ = mass ÷ density = 7.165 × 10⁻²² ÷ 10.5 = 6.824 × 10⁻²³ cm³, so a = 4.086 × 10⁻⁸ cm = 409 pm. FCC: r = a/(2√2) = 409/2.828 = 144 pm. Distractors: 409 pm is the edge length, not the radius; 204 pm is a/2; 177 pm uses the BCC relation; 91.0 pm uses 1 atom per cell.",
 ["Work backwards: density → cell volume → edge length → radius.","a³ = (4 × M / N_A) ÷ d; take the cube root, then convert cm to pm.","a = 409 pm; r = a/(2√2)."]],
["m10-a-093",10,22,4,"Exam","num",
 "A metal crystallizes in a body-centered cubic lattice with an edge length of 330.6 pm and has a density of 16.6 g/cm³. Calculate its molar mass. (Enter a number in g/mol.)",
 ["181 g/mol"],180.6,
 "d = (Z·M/N_A) ÷ a³, so M = d·a³·N_A ÷ Z. a = 3.306 × 10⁻⁸ cm, a³ = 3.613 × 10⁻²³ cm³. M = 16.6 × 3.613 × 10⁻²³ × 6.022 × 10²³ ÷ 2 = 361.2 ÷ 2 = 181 g/mol (the metal is tantalum, 180.95 g/mol). Using Z = 1 doubles it (361) and Z = 4 halves it (90.3).",
 ["Rearrange the density formula for M.","BCC: Z = 2. Cube the edge in cm.","M = 16.6 × (3.306 × 10⁻⁸)³ × 6.022 × 10²³ ÷ 2."]],
["m10-a-094",10,22,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "A metal has a face-centered cubic structure, a unit-cell edge of 392.4 pm and a density of 21.45 g/cm³. Which metal is it?",
 ["Pt","Ag","Cu","Pb","U"],0,
 "M = d·a³·N_A ÷ Z. a³ = (3.924 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³ = 6.042 × 10⁻²³ cm³. d·a³·N_A = 21.45 × 6.042 × 10⁻²³ × 6.022 × 10²³ = 780.5 g/mol per cell; ÷ 4 atoms = 195.1 g/mol, which matches Pt (195.08). Using Z = 2 (BCC) would give 390 g/mol and Z = 1 would give 780 g/mol - no real metals, a sign the cell count is wrong.",
 ["Find the molar mass first, then match it to the periodic table.","FCC means Z = 4.","M = 21.45 × (3.924 × 10⁻⁸)³ × 6.022 × 10²³ ÷ 4."]],
["m10-a-095",10,22,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "NaCl has a cubic unit cell with edge length 564.0 pm that contains 4 Na⁺ and 4 Cl⁻ ions. What is the density of NaCl?",
 ["0.541 g/cm³","1.08 g/cm³","2.16 g/cm³","4.33 g/cm³","0.851 g/cm³"],2,
 "The cell holds 4 formula units of NaCl (58.44 g/mol). Mass = 4 × 58.44 ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.882 × 10⁻²² g. a³ = (5.640 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³ = 1.794 × 10⁻²² cm³. Density = 2.16 g/cm³ (measured 2.17). Distractors: 0.541 uses 1 formula unit, 1.08 uses 2, 4.33 counts 8 formula units (adding the 4 + 4 ions as if each were a NaCl unit), and 0.851 uses only the mass of the 4 Na⁺ ions.",
 ["How many NaCl formula units are in the cell?","4 Na⁺ + 4 Cl⁻ = 4 NaCl units, each 58.44 g/mol.","3.882 × 10⁻²² g ÷ (5.640 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³."]],
["m10-a-096",10,22,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "In the NaCl (rock salt) structure, how many Cl⁻ ions surround each Na⁺ ion as nearest neighbours?",
 ["4","12","8","6","2"],3,
 "In rock salt each Na⁺ sits at the center of an octahedron of 6 Cl⁻ ions (one above, below, left, right, front, back), and each Cl⁻ is likewise surrounded by 6 Na⁺: coordination number 6 for both. The unit cell contains 4 NaCl formula units; 8 is the coordination number in CsCl and BCC metals; 12 is close packing.",
 ["Picture one Na⁺ in the rock-salt lattice and look along the three axes.","There are neighbours on both sides along x, y and z.","2 × 3 = 6."]],
["m10-a-097",10,22,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "What fraction of a corner atom belongs to one cubic unit cell?",
 ["½","¼","⅛","1","⅙"],2,
 "A corner point is shared by the 8 cubes that meet there, so each cell owns ⅛ of a corner atom. Face atoms are shared by 2 cells (½), edge atoms by 4 cells (¼), and an atom in the body center belongs entirely to one cell (1).",
 ["How many cubes meet at a single corner point?","Stack cubes in 3-D: 2 × 2 × 2 meet at a point.","Eight cells share it, so each owns 1/8."]],
["m10-a-098",10,22,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "A cubic unit cell has atoms of element X at every corner and atoms of element Y at the center of every face. What is the empirical formula of the compound?",
 ["XY","XY₂","X₈Y₆","X₂Y₃","XY₃"],4,
 "X: 8 corners × ⅛ = 1 atom per cell. Y: 6 faces × ½ = 3 atoms per cell. Ratio X : Y = 1 : 3, formula XY₃. X₈Y₆ counts the atoms drawn without the sharing fractions.",
 ["Apply the sharing fractions before taking a ratio.","Corners count ⅛, faces count ½.","1 X and 3 Y per cell."]],
["m10-a-099",10,22,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "In the perovskite structure, Ca atoms sit at the corners of a cube, a Ti atom sits at the body center, and O atoms sit at the face centers. What is the formula?",
 ["CaTiO₃","Ca₈TiO₆","CaTiO₆","Ca₂TiO₃","CaTi₂O₃"],0,
 "Ca: 8 corners × ⅛ = 1. Ti: 1 body center × 1 = 1. O: 6 faces × ½ = 3. Formula CaTiO₃ (calcium titanate). Check charges: Ca²⁺ + Ti⁴⁺ + 3 O²⁻ = 0. Counting drawn atoms gives Ca₈TiO₆; counting O as whole atoms gives CaTiO₆.",
 ["Use ⅛ for corners, ½ for faces and 1 for the body center.","Count each element separately.","1 Ca, 1 Ti, 3 O."]],
["m10-a-100",10,22,4,"Calculation","num",
 "Chromium crystallizes in a body-centered cubic lattice with an atomic radius of 125 pm. Calculate the density of chromium. (Enter a number in g/cm³.)",
 ["7.18 g/cm³"],7.179,
 "BCC: a = 4r/√3 = 4 × 125/1.7321 = 288.7 pm = 2.887 × 10⁻⁸ cm; a³ = 2.406 × 10⁻²³ cm³. Cell mass = 2 × 52.00 ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.727 × 10⁻²² g. Density = 7.18 g/cm³ (measured 7.19). Using a = 2r (simple cubic) would give 11.1 g/cm³.",
 ["BCC: 2 atoms per cell; atoms touch along the body diagonal.","a = 4r/√3, then convert to cm and cube.","d = (2 × 52.00 / 6.022 × 10²³) ÷ (2.887 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³."]],
["m10-a-101",10,22,4,"Calculation","num",
 "Nickel has a face-centered cubic structure and a density of 8.90 g/cm³. Calculate the atomic radius of Ni. (Enter a number in pm.)",
 ["125 pm"],124.6,
 "Cell mass = 4 × 58.69 ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.898 × 10⁻²² g. a³ = 3.898 × 10⁻²² ÷ 8.90 = 4.380 × 10⁻²³ cm³; a = 3.525 × 10⁻⁸ cm = 352.5 pm. FCC: r = a/(2√2) = 352.5 ÷ 2.828 = 125 pm (the tabulated value is 124 pm). The BCC relation would give 153 pm.",
 ["Density → a³ → a → r.","Z = 4 for FCC; a³ = (4 × 58.69 / N_A) ÷ 8.90.","a = 352.5 pm, then r = a/(2√2)."]],
["m10-a-102",10,22,4,"Calculation","mcq",
 "Polonium is the only element with a simple cubic structure. Its unit-cell edge is 335 pm and its molar mass is 209 g/mol. What is its density?",
 ["18.5 g/cm³","36.9 g/cm³","9.23 g/cm³","4.62 g/cm³","9.23 × 10⁻⁶ g/cm³"],2,
 "Simple cubic: 8 corners × ⅛ = 1 atom per cell. Mass = 209 ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.471 × 10⁻²² g. a³ = (3.35 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³ = 3.760 × 10⁻²³ cm³. Density = 9.23 g/cm³. Distractors use 2 atoms (18.5), 4 atoms (36.9), half an atom (4.62), or 1 pm taken as 10⁻⁸ cm, the ångström conversion (9.23 × 10⁻⁶).",
 ["How many atoms are in a simple cubic cell?","1 atom; cell volume = a³ in cm³.","209/6.022 × 10²³ g ÷ (3.35 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³."]],
["m10-a-103",10,22,3,"Calculation","num",
 "How many unit cells are in 1.00 g of iron, which is body-centered cubic? (Enter a number, e.g. 1.23e21.)",
 ["5.39 × 10²¹ unit cells"],5.391e21,
 "Atoms in 1.00 g = (1.00/55.85) mol × 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.078 × 10²² atoms. Each BCC cell contains 2 atoms, so cells = 1.078 × 10²² ÷ 2 = 5.39 × 10²¹. 1.08 × 10²² is the number of atoms (1 per cell); 2.70 × 10²¹ assumes 4 per cell (FCC); 1.79 × 10⁻² is the number of moles.",
 ["First find the number of Fe atoms in 1.00 g.","Then divide by atoms per BCC cell.","1.078 × 10²² ÷ 2."]],
["m10-a-104",10,22,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Diamond is best classified as which type of crystalline solid?",
 ["Molecular","Ionic","Metallic","Atomic","Network covalent"],4,
 "In diamond every C atom is covalently bonded to four others in a 3-D network that extends through the whole crystal - there are no separate molecules. That makes it a network covalent solid: extremely hard, with a very high melting point and no electrical conductivity. 'Atomic' solids are the noble gases, held only by dispersion forces.",
 ["Are there discrete molecules in diamond?","Each C forms four covalent bonds to other C atoms, without end.","A continuous covalent network."]],
["m10-a-105",10,22,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which pairing of solid and solid type is INCORRECT?",
 ["Ice - ionic","Cu - metallic","I₂ - molecular","SiO₂ (quartz) - network covalent","Ar - atomic"],0,
 "Ice is a MOLECULAR solid: H₂O molecules held in place by hydrogen bonds; there are no ions. The others are correct: I₂ molecules held by dispersion forces (molecular), Cu a lattice of cations in a sea of electrons (metallic), quartz an endless Si−O covalent network, and solid Ar individual atoms held by dispersion forces (atomic).",
 ["Ask what particles occupy the lattice points of each solid.","Is ice made of ions or of molecules?","Ice = H₂O molecules + hydrogen bonds: molecular."]],
["m10-a-106",10,22,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "A solid is shiny, malleable, and conducts electricity both as a solid and when molten. What type of solid is it most likely to be?",
 ["Ionic","Metallic","Molecular","Network covalent","Atomic"],1,
 "Conducting as a SOLID and being malleable are the signature of metallic bonding: delocalized valence electrons carry current and let layers of cations slide without breaking the bonding. Ionic solids conduct only when molten or dissolved (ions must be free to move) and are brittle; molecular, atomic and most network solids do not conduct.",
 ["Which solids conduct without melting?","Malleable rather than brittle points to non-directional bonding.","A sea of mobile electrons."]],
["m10-a-107",10,22,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "A white solid melts at 801 °C, is hard but brittle, does not conduct as a solid, but conducts when melted or dissolved in water. What type of solid is it?",
 ["Metallic","Molecular","Network covalent","Ionic","Atomic"],3,
 "High melting point + brittle + conducts only when the ions are free to move (melted or dissolved) = ionic solid (this is NaCl). Metals conduct as solids; molecular solids melt low; network covalent solids like quartz melt even higher but do not conduct when melted.",
 ["What must be free to move for an ionic compound to conduct?","Brittle and high-melting, conducting only in the liquid.","NaCl's properties."]],
["m10-a-108",10,22,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Graphite is a network covalent solid, yet it is soft, slippery and conducts electricity. Why?",
 ["Graphite is actually a metal","Its carbon atoms form ionic bonds that break easily","It consists of flat sheets of covalently bonded C atoms with delocalized π electrons within each sheet; the sheets are held to each other only by dispersion forces","Its carbon atoms are held together only by dispersion forces","Graphite is amorphous, so it has no fixed structure"],2,
 "Within each layer every C is covalently bonded to three others in hexagonal rings, and the fourth electron of each C is delocalized across the layer, so graphite conducts along the sheets. Between the layers there are only weak dispersion forces, so the sheets slide over one another (pencils, lubricants). Diamond, with all four electrons in localized σ bonds in 3-D, is hard and non-conducting.",
 ["Think about the bonding within a layer versus between layers.","Each C in graphite bonds to only three others. Where does its fourth electron go?","Strong covalent sheets, delocalized electrons, weak dispersion between sheets."]],
["m10-a-109",10,22,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "How does an amorphous solid such as glass differ from a crystalline solid such as quartz?",
 ["Amorphous solids have a sharp melting point","Amorphous solids are made of molecules, crystalline solids of atoms","Amorphous solids are always ionic","Crystalline solids have no repeating unit cell","Amorphous solids have no long-range order and soften gradually over a range of temperatures"],4,
 "A crystalline solid repeats one unit cell throughout, so all its interactions are identical and it melts sharply at one temperature. An amorphous solid (glass, many plastics) has particles arranged randomly - short-range order at most - so its interactions vary in strength and it softens over a temperature range. Glass and quartz are both SiO₂; the difference is order, not composition.",
 ["Order versus disorder at the particle level.","What happens to a material with many different interaction strengths when heated?","No long-range order; it softens over a range."]],
["m10-a-110",10,22,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank these solids in order of INCREASING melting point: NaCl, diamond, Ar, ice (H₂O).",
 ["Ar < ice < NaCl < diamond","Ice < Ar < NaCl < diamond","Ar < NaCl < ice < diamond","Diamond < NaCl < ice < Ar","Ar < ice < diamond < NaCl"],0,
 "Melting point follows the strength of what holds the lattice together. Ar (atomic; weak dispersion) melts at −189 °C. Ice (molecular; hydrogen bonds) melts at 0 °C. NaCl (ionic; strong electrostatic attraction) melts at 801 °C. Diamond (network covalent; every atom covalently bonded) does not melt until well above 3500 °C. Atomic < molecular < ionic < network covalent is the usual ranking.",
 ["Classify each solid by its type.","Dispersion < hydrogen bonds < ionic bonds < a covalent network.","Atomic, molecular, ionic, network."]],
["m10-a-111",10,22,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which property is characteristic of a MOLECULAR solid such as I₂ or solid CO₂?",
 ["Very high melting point","Electrical conductivity as a solid","Relatively soft, with a low melting point","Malleability","Conductivity when melted"],2,
 "Molecular solids are held together by intermolecular forces (dispersion, dipole-dipole, hydrogen bonds), which are much weaker than ionic, metallic or covalent-network bonding, so they are soft and melt or sublime at low temperatures. They contain no ions or free electrons, so they do not conduct, solid or melted.",
 ["What holds a molecular solid together?","Those forces are weak compared with bonds.","Soft and low-melting."]],
["m10-a-112",10,22,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement about crystalline solids is FALSE?",
 ["Quartz (SiO₂) and diamond are network covalent solids with very high melting points","Ionic solids conduct electricity when molten but not as solids","Metallic solids conduct electricity because of delocalized valence electrons","Molecular solids generally have higher melting points than ionic solids because molecules are larger than ions","Solid argon is an atomic solid held together by dispersion forces"],3,
 "Molecular solids are held by intermolecular forces, which are far weaker than the ionic attractions in an ionic lattice, so they usually melt much LOWER (I₂ 114 °C vs NaCl 801 °C). Size does not overturn that. The other statements are correct descriptions of network, ionic, metallic and atomic solids.",
 ["Compare the forces that must be overcome to melt each type of solid.","Intermolecular forces vs ionic bonds - which is stronger?","Molecular solids melt lower, not higher."]],
["m10-a-113",10,22,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "For a simple cubic arrangement of identical spheres of radius r, the packing efficiency is (volume of one sphere) ÷ (volume of the cube, a = 2r). What value does this give?",
 ["π/6 ≈ 52%","π/4 ≈ 79%","√3π/8 ≈ 68%","π/(3√2) ≈ 74%","1/8 ≈ 12.5%"],0,
 "One atom per cell: volume (4/3)πr³. Cell volume a³ = (2r)³ = 8r³. Fraction = (4/3)πr³ ÷ 8r³ = π/6 = 0.524, about 52%. √3π/8 (68%) is BCC and π/(3√2) (74%) is FCC/close packing; 1/8 counts only the corner fraction, not the volume.",
 ["Simple cubic has one atom per cell and a = 2r.","Divide (4/3)πr³ by (2r)³.","(4/3)π ÷ 8 = π/6."]],
]);

/* ---------------- Gym 11 · Lesson 23: IMFs in solution; biological macromolecules ---------------- */
chemAddQuestions([
["m11-a-001",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which substance is the MOST soluble in hexane, C₆H₁₄?",
 ["NaCl","KNO₃","CH₃OH","Glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆","I₂"],4,
 "Like dissolves like. Hexane is nonpolar and attracts solutes only through dispersion forces. I₂ is nonpolar, so I₂-hexane dispersion forces can replace the I₂-I₂ and hexane-hexane attractions (I₂ dissolves to give a violet solution). The ionic salts and the hydrogen-bonding molecules (methanol, glucose) would have to give up strong ion-ion or H-bond attractions with nothing comparable in return.",
 ["Is hexane polar or nonpolar?","Which solute is held together only by dispersion forces?","Nonpolar dissolves nonpolar."]],
["m11-a-002",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which substance is the MOST soluble in water?",
 ["CH₄","CCl₄","C₆H₁₄","CH₃OH","I₂"],3,
 "Methanol has an O−H group, so it hydrogen bonds with water and mixes with it in all proportions. CH₄, CCl₄, hexane and I₂ are nonpolar: they can offer water only weak dispersion/dipole-induced dipole attractions, which cannot make up for the water-water hydrogen bonds that would have to break.",
 ["Like dissolves like - water is polar and hydrogen bonds.","Which solute can hydrogen bond with water?","The one with an O−H group."]],
["m11-a-003",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which alcohol is the LEAST soluble in water?",
 ["Methanol, CH₃OH","Ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH","1-Propanol, CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","1-Butanol, CH₃(CH₂)₃OH","1-Hexanol, CH₃(CH₂)₅OH"],4,
 "Every alcohol has one O−H group that hydrogen bonds to water, but the hydrocarbon chain is nonpolar and interacts only weakly with water. As the chain lengthens, the nonpolar part dominates: methanol, ethanol and 1-propanol are miscible with water, 1-butanol dissolves only about 7 g per 100 mL, and 1-hexanol only about 0.6 g per 100 mL. The longest chain is least soluble.",
 ["Every choice has one O−H. What is different?","The chain is nonpolar and gets longer down the list.","The longest nonpolar tail is least water-soluble."]],
["m11-a-004",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank these alcohols in order of INCREASING solubility in water: 1-butanol, methanol, 1-pentanol, 1-propanol.",
 ["1-Pentanol < 1-butanol < 1-propanol < methanol","Methanol < 1-propanol < 1-butanol < 1-pentanol","1-Butanol < 1-pentanol < methanol < 1-propanol","1-Propanol < methanol < 1-butanol < 1-pentanol","All four are equally soluble because each has one O−H group"],0,
 "Water solubility falls as the nonpolar hydrocarbon portion grows relative to the single O−H. Least soluble: 1-pentanol (5 C), then 1-butanol (4 C), then 1-propanol (3 C, miscible), then methanol (1 C, miscible, the most 'water-like'). Reversing the order equates 'bigger molecule' with 'stronger attraction to water' - but the bigger part is the nonpolar part.",
 ["Count carbons in each chain.","More CH₂ groups = more nonpolar surface that water cannot H-bond to.","Longest chain least soluble, shortest most soluble."]],
["m11-a-005",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "When ethanol dissolves in water, which solute-solvent attraction is MOST important?",
 ["Hydrogen bonding","Ion-dipole","Dispersion only","Ion-induced dipole","Covalent bonding"],0,
 "Ethanol's O−H group hydrogen bonds with water (ethanol can both donate and accept). Those ethanol-water H-bonds replace the ethanol-ethanol and water-water H-bonds broken during mixing, which is why the two are miscible. There are no ions, and dispersion forces, though present, are not the dominant attraction.",
 ["Both liquids have O−H groups.","What force connects an O−H on one molecule to a lone pair on another?","Hydrogen bonding."]],
["m11-a-006",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Hexane dissolves completely in octane. Which attractive force acts between the solute and solvent molecules?",
 ["Hydrogen bonding","Dipole-dipole","Ion-dipole","Dispersion forces","Dipole-induced dipole"],3,
 "Hexane and octane are both nonpolar hydrocarbons, so the only attractions available - solute-solute, solvent-solvent and solute-solvent - are dispersion forces. The new hexane-octane attractions are about as strong as the ones they replace, and the increase in entropy on mixing drives the solution to form.",
 ["Are either of these molecules polar?","With no dipoles and no ions, what force is left?","Dispersion."]],
["m11-a-007",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "The process in which ions or molecules of a solute become surrounded by water molecules is called:",
 ["hydration","hydrolysis","dehydration","condensation","sublimation"],0,
 "Solvation is the surrounding of solute particles by solvent molecules; when the solvent is water it is called hydration (each Na⁺ in water carries a shell of water molecules with their O ends inward). Hydrolysis is a REACTION with water that breaks a bond; dehydration removes water.",
 ["Solvation by water has its own name.","It is not a reaction - no bonds in the solute break.","Hydr- + -ation."]],
["m11-a-008",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Methanol (CH₃OH) mixes with water in all proportions, while 1-octanol (CH₃(CH₂)₇OH) is nearly insoluble. What is the best explanation?",
 ["1-Octanol cannot hydrogen bond","Methanol is ionic","1-Octanol's long nonpolar hydrocarbon chain cannot hydrogen bond with water, and it dominates the molecule's interactions","1-Octanol is too heavy to dissolve","Methanol reacts with water"],2,
 "Both alcohols have one O−H that hydrogen bonds with water. In methanol the O−H is most of the molecule; in 1-octanol it is attached to an 8-carbon nonpolar tail. Water molecules next to that tail lose hydrogen bonds and gain only weak dispersion attractions, so dissolving is unfavourable. Mass alone does not prevent dissolving (sucrose, 342 g/mol, is very soluble).",
 ["Both alcohols have an O−H. What else is in each molecule?","What kind of attraction can a CH₂ chain offer water?","The long nonpolar tail outweighs the single O−H."]],
["m11-a-009",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which pair of liquids would you expect to be MISCIBLE (mix in all proportions)?",
 ["Water and hexane","Water and carbon tetrachloride","Water and octane","Water and acetone","Methanol and hexane at room temperature, because both are liquids"],3,
 "Acetone, (CH₃)₂C=O, is polar and its O accepts hydrogen bonds from water, so the two are miscible. Hexane, CCl₄ and octane are nonpolar and form separate layers with water. Methanol and hexane are only partly miscible at room temperature - being liquids does not guarantee mixing; the IMFs must be similar.",
 ["Look for a partner to water that is polar and can H-bond.","Acetone has a C=O whose oxygen can accept an H-bond.","Water + acetone."]],
["m11-a-010",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Vitamin A is mostly a long hydrocarbon chain with a single O−H group; vitamin C (C₆H₈O₆) is a small molecule with four O−H groups. Which statement is correct?",
 ["Both are water-soluble because both contain O−H groups","Vitamin A is water-soluble; vitamin C is fat-soluble","Vitamin A is fat-soluble (dissolves in nonpolar body fat); vitamin C is water-soluble","Both are fat-soluble","Neither dissolves in anything"],2,
 "Vitamin A, like 1-octanol, is dominated by its nonpolar chain, so it dissolves in nonpolar fatty tissue and is stored there (excess can accumulate). Vitamin C's many O−H groups hydrogen bond extensively with water, so it is water-soluble and excess is excreted. A single O−H on a large hydrocarbon does not make a molecule water-soluble.",
 ["Compare the polar part of each molecule with its nonpolar part.","Body fat is nonpolar; blood is mostly water.","Long chain + one OH → fat; small molecule + many OH → water."]],
["m11-a-011",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "In hemoglobin, an O₂ molecule binds to an Fe²⁺ ion. Which kind of intermolecular attraction describes Fe²⁺ attracting nonpolar O₂?",
 ["Ion-dipole","Dipole-dipole","Hydrogen bonding","Ion-induced dipole","Dispersion between two nonpolar molecules"],3,
 "O₂ is nonpolar, so it has no permanent dipole for an ion to attract. The charge of Fe²⁺ distorts O₂'s electron cloud and induces a dipole, and the ion attracts that induced dipole: an ion-induced dipole force. Ion-dipole would require a polar partner such as water.",
 ["One partner carries a full charge; the other is nonpolar.","Can a charge distort a nonpolar electron cloud?","The ion induces a dipole in O₂."]],
["m11-a-012",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "The enthalpy of solution can be thought of as three steps: separating solute particles (ΔH(solute)), separating solvent particles (ΔH(solvent)) and mixing them (ΔH(mix)). Which step is EXOTHERMIC?",
 ["Separating the solute particles","Separating the solvent particles","None of the steps","All three steps","Mixing the separated solute and solvent particles"],4,
 "Pulling particles apart against their attractions always costs energy, so ΔH(solute) > 0 and ΔH(solvent) > 0. Bringing solute and solvent together forms new attractions and releases energy, so ΔH(mix) < 0. ΔH(soln) = ΔH(solute) + ΔH(solvent) + ΔH(mix); its sign depends on whether the release in mixing beats the two costs.",
 ["Separating attracted particles - does that absorb or release energy?","Forming new attractions releases energy.","Only the mixing step forms attractions."]],
["m11-a-013",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "A solid dissolves in water and the solution becomes noticeably COLDER. Which statement is true?",
 ["ΔH(soln) < 0, so ΔH(mix) is larger in magnitude than ΔH(solute) + ΔH(solvent)","ΔH(soln) > 0, because the energy released in mixing is smaller than the energy needed to separate solute and solvent particles","No intermolecular forces were broken","The solute must be nonpolar","The process cannot be spontaneous, because it is endothermic"],1,
 "The solution cooled, so the process absorbed heat from its surroundings: endothermic, ΔH(soln) > 0. That happens when |ΔH(mix)| < ΔH(solute) + ΔH(solvent). It still happens on its own because dissolving increases entropy (the particles become far more dispersed). NH₄NO₃ in a cold pack is the standard example.",
 ["Getting colder means heat was absorbed from the water.","Endothermic dissolving: costs beat payback.","ΔH(soln) > 0, driven by entropy."]],
["m11-a-014",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Instant cold packs contain solid NH₄NO₃ and a pouch of water; instant hot packs often use CaCl₂. Which statement is correct?",
 ["Dissolving NH₄NO₃ is endothermic (ΔH(soln) > 0) and dissolving CaCl₂ is exothermic (ΔH(soln) < 0)","Both salts dissolve exothermically","Dissolving NH₄NO₃ is exothermic and dissolving CaCl₂ is endothermic","NH₄NO₃ does not dissolve; the cooling comes from a chemical reaction with water","The pack temperatures depend only on the amount of water, not on the salt"],0,
 "In a cold pack, dissolving NH₄NO₃ absorbs heat from the water (ΔH(soln) ≈ +25.7 kJ/mol), so the pack chills. In a hot pack, CaCl₂'s small, doubly charged Ca²⁺ ion is hydrated so strongly that ΔH(mix) outweighs the lattice cost (ΔH(soln) ≈ −81 kJ/mol), releasing heat. Both are physical dissolving, not reactions.",
 ["Cold pack = absorbs heat from its surroundings.","Absorbs heat → endothermic → ΔH > 0.","NH₄NO₃ endothermic, CaCl₂ exothermic."]],
["m11-a-015",11,23,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "For NaCl, the energy needed to separate the solid into gaseous ions is +787 kJ/mol, and the hydration of the gaseous Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions releases 783 kJ/mol. What is ΔH(soln) of NaCl?",
 ["−4 kJ/mol","0 kJ/mol","+1570 kJ/mol","+4 kJ/mol","−1570 kJ/mol"],3,
 "For an ionic solid, ΔH(soln) = ΔH(lattice) + ΔH(hydration) = (+787) + (−783) = +4 kJ/mol: slightly endothermic, which is why dissolving salt barely changes the temperature of water. −4 kJ/mol gets one sign wrong; ±1570 kJ/mol adds the magnitudes instead of letting the release cancel the cost.",
 ["Separating the lattice costs energy (+); hydration releases energy (−).","ΔH(soln) = ΔH(lattice) + ΔH(hydr).","787 − 783."]],
["m11-a-016",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "NH₄NO₃ dissolves readily in water even though ΔH(soln) > 0. What drives the process?",
 ["The formation of covalent bonds between NH₄⁺ and water","An increase in entropy: the ions and water become much more dispersed and disordered in solution","A decrease in the energy of the system","The heat released by hydration, which makes the solution warmer","Gravity pulling the dense solid apart"],1,
 "Two tendencies decide whether a process happens on its own: lower energy (enthalpy) and greater dispersal of matter and energy (entropy). Dissolving NH₄NO₃ raises the enthalpy, but scattering an ordered lattice of ions through the solvent greatly increases entropy, and that wins. (The solution gets colder, not warmer.)",
 ["If the energy goes UP, something else must favour dissolving.","Think about order and disorder.","Entropy increases when a lattice disperses into solution."]],
["m11-a-017",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "How does the solubility of a gas such as O₂ in water change as the water temperature increases?",
 ["It increases","It decreases","It stays the same","It increases, then decreases","It depends only on pressure, not temperature"],1,
 "Dissolving a gas is exothermic (gas molecules give up kinetic freedom and form attractions with the solvent), and warmer solvent gives dissolved molecules more energy to escape. So gas solubility FALLS as temperature rises - the opposite of most solids. Warm soda goes flat faster, and 'thermal pollution' from warm discharge water leaves fish short of O₂.",
 ["Think about a warm soda versus a cold one.","Hotter molecules escape the liquid more easily.","Gas solubility drops as T rises."]],
["m11-a-018",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "According to Henry's law, if the partial pressure of CO₂ above a solution is doubled at constant temperature, the solubility of CO₂:",
 ["is halved","stays the same","doubles","quadruples","increases by a factor of √2"],2,
 "Henry's law: S(gas) = k(H)·P(gas). Solubility is directly proportional to the partial pressure of the gas above the liquid, so doubling P doubles S. Halving would be an inverse relationship; Henry's law is linear.",
 ["Write Henry's law.","S = k(H)·P - how does S change if P doubles?","Directly proportional."]],
["m11-a-019",11,23,3,"Calculation","num",
 "The Henry's law constant for O₂ in water at 25 °C is 1.3 × 10⁻³ mol/(L·atm). What is the solubility of O₂ in water exposed to air, where the partial pressure of O₂ is 0.21 atm? (Enter a number in mol/L.)",
 ["2.7 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L"],2.73e-4,
 "S = k(H)·P(O₂) = 1.3 × 10⁻³ mol/(L·atm) × 0.21 atm = 2.7 × 10⁻⁴ M. Use the PARTIAL pressure of O₂ (21% of 1 atm), not the total pressure of air; using 1 atm gives 1.3 × 10⁻³ M.",
 ["Henry's law uses the partial pressure of the gas.","S = k(H) × P.","1.3 × 10⁻³ × 0.21."],{tol:0.02}],
["m11-a-020",11,23,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "A soft drink is bottled under a CO₂ pressure of 4.0 atm. After opening, it sits in air where the partial pressure of CO₂ is 3.0 × 10⁻⁴ atm. k(H) for CO₂ = 3.3 × 10⁻² mol/(L·atm). What are the CO₂ solubilities in the sealed bottle and in the opened drink at equilibrium?",
 ["0.13 M sealed; 9.9 × 10⁻⁶ M opened","0.13 M sealed; 0.13 M opened","8.3 × 10⁻³ M sealed; 1.1 × 10² M opened","1.2 × 10² M sealed; 9.1 × 10⁻³ M opened","3.3 × 10⁻² M sealed; 3.3 × 10⁻² M opened"],0,
 "S = k(H)·P. Sealed: 3.3 × 10⁻² × 4.0 = 0.13 M. Opened: 3.3 × 10⁻² × 3.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 9.9 × 10⁻⁶ M. The drop of more than 10 000-fold is why the drink goes flat. the 8.3 × 10⁻³ M / 1.1 × 10² M pair divides k by P and the 1.2 × 10² M / 9.1 × 10⁻³ M pair divides P by k instead of multiplying; 3.3 × 10⁻² M ignores the pressure altogether.",
 ["Henry's law for each case: S = k(H)·P.","Use 4.0 atm for the sealed bottle and the tiny atmospheric CO₂ pressure once opened.","3.3 × 10⁻² × 4.0, and 3.3 × 10⁻² × 3.0 × 10⁻⁴."]],
["m11-a-021",11,23,4,"Calculation","num",
 "k(H) for N₂ in water at 25 °C is 6.8 × 10⁻⁴ mol/(L·atm). What is the solubility of N₂ when its partial pressure is 588 torr? (Enter a number in mol/L.)",
 ["5.3 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L"],5.26e-4,
 "First convert the pressure to the units of k(H): 588 torr × (1 atm/760 torr) = 0.7737 atm. Then S = 6.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.7737 = 5.3 × 10⁻⁴ M. Plugging 588 straight in gives 0.40 M - a solubility nearly 800 times too large, the classic unit trap.",
 ["Check the pressure unit in k(H).","Convert torr to atm: ÷ 760.","S = 6.8 × 10⁻⁴ × (588/760)."]],
["m11-a-022",11,23,2,"Calculation","mcq",
 "At 1.0 atm of a gas above water, the gas solubility is 0.034 M. At the same temperature, what is its solubility at 2.5 atm?",
 ["0.014 M","0.034 M","0.21 M","0.085 M","0.0085 M"],3,
 "Henry's law makes S proportional to P, so S₂ = S₁ × (P₂/P₁) = 0.034 M × (2.5/1.0) = 0.085 M. 0.014 M divides by 2.5 instead of multiplying (more pressure must push MORE gas in); 0.034 M ignores the pressure change; 0.21 M applies the factor of 2.5 twice; 0.0085 M is a decimal-place slip.",
 ["S/P is constant at fixed temperature.","S₂ = S₁ × P₂/P₁.","0.034 × 2.5."]],
["m11-a-023",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why does a carbonated drink fizz when it is opened?",
 ["Opening lets in O₂, which reacts with the CO₂","The pressure of CO₂ above the liquid drops, so CO₂ solubility drops (Henry's law) and the excess gas comes out of solution","Opening the bottle raises the temperature of the drink sharply","CO₂ becomes more soluble at lower pressure","The sugar decomposes when exposed to air"],1,
 "Sealed, the drink is in equilibrium with CO₂ at several atmospheres. Opened, the CO₂ partial pressure above it falls to almost zero, so by S = k(H)·P the equilibrium solubility collapses and the dissolved excess escapes as bubbles. No chemical reaction is involved.",
 ["Which variable in Henry's law changes when you open the cap?","Lower P means lower solubility.","The extra dissolved CO₂ bubbles out."]],
["m11-a-024",11,23,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "Using k(H) = 1.3 × 10⁻³ mol/(L·atm) for O₂ at 25 °C and P(O₂) = 0.21 atm, how many milligrams of O₂ dissolve in 1.00 L of water?",
 ["4.4 mg","41.6 mg","0.27 mg","8.7 mg","2.7 × 10⁻⁴ mg"],3,
 "S = 1.3 × 10⁻³ × 0.21 = 2.73 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L. Mass = 2.73 × 10⁻⁴ mol × 32.00 g/mol = 8.74 × 10⁻³ g = 8.7 mg in 1.00 L (healthy natural water has 8-9 mg/L). 4.4 mg uses 16.00 g/mol (O atoms, not O₂); 41.6 mg uses 1 atm instead of the partial pressure; 0.27 mg and 2.7 × 10⁻⁴ mg come from unit slips (mmol read as mg, or moles reported as mg).",
 ["Henry's law gives mol/L; then convert to grams.","Use the molar mass of O₂, 32.00 g/mol, and the partial pressure.","2.73 × 10⁻⁴ mol × 32.00 g/mol, in mg."]],
["m11-a-025",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Soap molecules (for example sodium stearate, CH₃(CH₂)₁₆COO⁻ Na⁺) remove grease from your hands because:",
 ["the ionic head dissolves in grease and the hydrocarbon tail dissolves in water","soap raises the surface tension of water","soap reacts covalently with grease to form a new polar compound","soap molecules are entirely nonpolar","the long nonpolar tail dissolves in the grease, while the charged −COO⁻ head interacts with water through ion-dipole forces"],4,
 "Soap is amphipathic: one end is nonpolar and one end is ionic. The hydrocarbon tails mix with nonpolar grease via dispersion forces, while the carboxylate heads stay in the water, attracted by ion-dipole forces. The tails cluster around a grease droplet with the heads facing out (a micelle), and the whole droplet washes away. Soap lowers, rather than raises, water's surface tension.",
 ["Each end of a soap molecule has a different polarity.","Which end resembles grease and which resembles a salt?","Tail in the grease, head in the water."]],
["m11-a-026",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "A cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer. How are the phospholipid molecules arranged?",
 ["Polar (charged) heads face the water inside and outside the cell; nonpolar hydrocarbon tails point toward each other in the middle of the bilayer","Nonpolar tails face the water; polar heads are buried in the middle","Heads and tails alternate randomly","The molecules are covalently bonded into one giant molecule","Every molecule lies flat on the surface of the cell"],0,
 "Like soap, a phospholipid has a polar/ionic head and two long nonpolar tails. In water, the most favourable arrangement puts the heads in contact with water (ion-dipole, H-bonding) and hides the tails from it, against each other (dispersion forces). Two layers back to back give the bilayer. It is held together by IMFs, not covalent bonds between molecules, which keeps the membrane flexible.",
 ["Which part of a phospholipid is attracted to water?","Water is on both sides of a membrane.","Heads out toward water, tails tucked inside."]],
["m11-a-027",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Proteins are polymers built from which monomers?",
 ["Monosaccharides","Nucleotides","Amino acids","Fatty acids","Phosphate ions"],2,
 "Proteins (polypeptides) are long chains of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. Monosaccharides build polysaccharides (starch, cellulose); nucleotides build nucleic acids (DNA, RNA); fatty acids are parts of fats and phospholipids, which are not polymers in the same sense.",
 ["Poly-peptide: what is joined?","Each unit has an amine group and a carboxylic acid group.","Amino acids."]],
["m11-a-028",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "A peptide bond forms when:",
 ["the −COOH group of one amino acid reacts with the −NH₂ group of another, forming an amide bond (−CO−NH−) and releasing H₂O","two amino acids share an S−S bond","two amino acids are held together by hydrogen bonds","a sugar joins a phosphate group","an amino acid gains a proton"],0,
 "A peptide bond is a covalent amide linkage made in a condensation (dehydration) reaction: the carboxyl carbon of one amino acid bonds to the amine nitrogen of the next, and one molecule of water is lost. S−S bonds are disulfide bridges between cysteine side chains; hydrogen bonds hold secondary structure, not the chain itself.",
 ["A peptide bond is covalent - what two groups react?","A small molecule is released, so it is a condensation reaction.","−COOH + H₂N− → −CO−NH− + H₂O."]],
["m11-a-029",11,23,2,"Calculation","mcq",
 "A protein chain contains 150 amino acids. How many peptide bonds does it contain, and how many water molecules were released in forming it?",
 ["150 peptide bonds; 150 H₂O","149 peptide bonds; 149 H₂O","150 peptide bonds; 149 H₂O","149 peptide bonds; 150 H₂O","75 peptide bonds; 75 H₂O"],1,
 "In a linear chain of n units there are n − 1 links: 150 amino acids are joined by 149 peptide bonds. Each peptide bond forms by condensation with the loss of one H₂O, so 149 water molecules are released. Counting one bond per amino acid is the fencepost error.",
 ["How many links join 3 beads on a string?","n units in a line need n − 1 links.","Each link releases one water."]],
["m11-a-030",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "The PRIMARY structure of a protein is:",
 ["its α-helices and β-sheets","its overall three-dimensional fold","the sequence of amino acids, held together by covalent peptide bonds","the association of several polypeptide chains","the hydrogen bonds between side chains"],2,
 "Primary structure = the order of amino acids along the chain (e.g., Gly-Ala-Ser...), fixed by covalent peptide bonds. Secondary structure is local folding (α-helix, β-sheet) held by backbone H-bonds; tertiary is the overall 3-D shape of one chain; quaternary is the arrangement of several chains.",
 ["'Primary' = the first, most basic level.","Before any folding, what defines a protein?","Its amino acid sequence."]],
["m11-a-031",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "What holds a protein's SECONDARY structure (α-helix and β-pleated sheet) together?",
 ["Disulfide bonds between cysteine side chains","Covalent peptide bonds only","Ionic attractions between charged side chains","Hydrogen bonds between the C=O of one peptide group and the N−H of another along the backbone","Dispersion forces between nonpolar side chains"],3,
 "In an α-helix, each backbone C=O hydrogen bonds to the N−H four residues further along the same chain, coiling it; in a β-sheet, the C=O and N−H groups of neighbouring strands hydrogen bond side by side. These are BACKBONE interactions. Disulfide, ionic and dispersion interactions between side chains mainly stabilize tertiary structure.",
 ["Secondary structure involves the backbone, not the side chains.","The backbone has C=O and N−H groups - which IMF can link them?","Backbone hydrogen bonds."]],
["m11-a-032",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which set lists the interactions that stabilize a protein's TERTIARY structure?",
 ["Peptide bonds only","Backbone hydrogen bonds only","Side-chain interactions: hydrogen bonds, ionic attractions (salt bridges), dispersion forces between nonpolar groups, and covalent disulfide (S−S) bonds","Base pairing between A-T and G-C","Glycosidic bonds between sugar units"],2,
 "Tertiary structure - the overall 3-D fold of one chain - comes from interactions between R groups (side chains) that may be far apart in the sequence: H-bonds between polar side chains, ionic attractions between −COO⁻ and −NH₃⁺ groups, dispersion forces between nonpolar side chains clustered inside the protein, and covalent disulfide bridges between cysteines. Base pairing is DNA; glycosidic bonds join sugars.",
 ["Tertiary structure is about the side chains (R groups).","There are four kinds of side-chain interaction - three IMFs and one covalent bond.","H-bonds, salt bridges, dispersion, disulfides."]],
["m11-a-033",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which interaction that stabilizes a protein's tertiary structure is a COVALENT bond?",
 ["A hydrogen bond between two serine −OH groups","A salt bridge between −COO⁻ and −NH₃⁺","Dispersion forces between two leucine side chains","A disulfide bond between two cysteine side chains","A dipole-dipole attraction between two polar side chains"],3,
 "Two cysteine side chains (−CH₂−SH) can be oxidized to form a disulfide bridge, −CH₂−S−S−CH₂−, a true covalent bond that can cross-link distant parts of a chain (or two chains). The other four are intermolecular (non-covalent) forces, individually much weaker.",
 ["Only one of these involves shared electron pairs between atoms.","Which amino acid has an −SH group?","S−S between cysteines."]],
["m11-a-034",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Side chains: lysine −(CH₂)₄NH₃⁺, aspartate −CH₂COO⁻, serine −CH₂OH, valine −CH(CH₃)₂, cysteine −CH₂SH. Which two side chains can form a salt bridge (ionic attraction)?",
 ["Serine and valine","Valine and cysteine","Cysteine and cysteine","Serine and aspartate","Lysine and aspartate"],4,
 "A salt bridge is an ionic attraction between a positively charged side chain and a negatively charged one. Lysine carries −NH₃⁺ and aspartate carries −COO⁻ at body pH. Serine and aspartate could hydrogen bond; two valines interact by dispersion; two cysteines form a disulfide (covalent) bond.",
 ["You need one positive and one negative side chain.","Find the −NH₃⁺ and the −COO⁻.","Lysine (+) with aspartate (−)."]],
["m11-a-035",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "A water-soluble globular protein folds in water. Where are side chains such as valine, leucine and phenylalanine (nonpolar hydrocarbon groups) usually found?",
 ["On the outer surface, in contact with water","Removed from the chain during folding","Covalently bonded to water molecules","Paired with the charged side chains on the surface","Buried in the interior of the protein, clustered together by dispersion forces"],4,
 "Nonpolar side chains cannot hydrogen bond with water, so the folded protein buries them in its core, where they attract each other through dispersion forces (the 'hydrophobic effect'). Polar and charged side chains (serine, lysine, aspartate) end up on the surface, where they hydrogen bond or form ion-dipole interactions with water. It is like dissolves like at the scale of one molecule.",
 ["Like dissolves like applies inside a protein too.","Would a hydrocarbon group rather touch water or another hydrocarbon group?","Nonpolar inside, polar outside."]],
["m11-a-036",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "When an egg white is cooked, its proteins are denatured. Which level of protein structure remains intact?",
 ["Primary structure","Secondary structure","Tertiary structure","Quaternary structure","None - every level is destroyed"],0,
 "Moderate heat gives molecules enough energy to break the weak H-bonds, salt bridges and dispersion interactions that hold secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure, so the protein unfolds and loses function. The covalent peptide bonds of the primary structure are not broken by cooking; breaking them (hydrolysis) needs strong acid or enzymes.",
 ["Which level is held by covalent bonds?","Cooking temperatures break IMFs, not peptide bonds.","The amino acid sequence survives."]],
["m11-a-037",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which attraction holds two valine side chains, −CH(CH₃)₂, close together inside a folded protein?",
 ["Hydrogen bonding","Ionic attraction","Dispersion forces","Disulfide bonding","Ion-dipole forces"],2,
 "Valine's side chain is a nonpolar hydrocarbon group: no charge, no O−H or N−H, no sulfur. The only attraction between two such groups is London dispersion. Many such contacts together contribute substantially to the stability of a protein's core.",
 ["Is the valine side chain charged or polar?","A pure hydrocarbon group has only one IMF available.","Dispersion."]],
["m11-a-038",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Hemoglobin consists of four polypeptide chains packed together. This level of organization is called:",
 ["primary structure","secondary structure","tertiary structure","quaternary structure","a β-pleated sheet"],3,
 "Quaternary structure is the arrangement of two or more separate polypeptide chains (subunits) in a functional protein, held by the same kinds of side-chain interactions as tertiary structure. Proteins made of a single chain have no quaternary level.",
 ["This level only exists when there is more than one chain.","Count the levels: sequence, local folds, whole-chain fold, then...","Several chains together = quaternary."]],
["m11-a-039",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Starch, glycogen and cellulose are all polymers of:",
 ["glucose","nucleotides","amino acids","fatty acids","glycerol"],0,
 "All three are polysaccharides made of glucose units. They differ in how the glucose units are linked and branched: starch (plants) and glycogen (animals) are energy-storage forms; cellulose is the structural material of plant cell walls.",
 ["Poly-saccharide = many sugars.","Which sugar is the most common monomer?","Glucose."]],
["m11-a-040",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Starch and cellulose are both made of glucose units. Which statement correctly contrasts them?",
 ["Starch is structural in plant cell walls; cellulose stores energy","Starch is an energy-storage polysaccharide that humans digest; cellulose is a structural polysaccharide whose different glucose linkages humans cannot digest","Cellulose contains nitrogen and starch does not","Starch is a protein and cellulose is a carbohydrate","They have identical linkages, so they behave identically"],1,
 "Starch links glucose units with α-linkages that form helical, digestible chains - plants' energy store, and a major food. Cellulose uses β-linkages, giving straight chains that line up and hydrogen bond to each other through their many O−H groups into strong fibers (wood, cotton). Human enzymes cannot break β-linkages, so cellulose passes through as dietary fiber. Neither contains nitrogen.",
 ["Same monomer, different linkage, different job.","Which one do we eat for energy? Which one is wood and cotton?","Starch = storage (digestible); cellulose = structure (not digestible)."]],
["m11-a-041",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Cellulose fibers (cotton, wood) are strong and insoluble in water even though every glucose unit carries several O−H groups. What best explains this?",
 ["Cellulose is nonpolar","Cellulose has no O−H groups available","Cellulose chains are joined by ionic bonds","The straight cellulose chains line up side by side and hydrogen bond extensively to EACH OTHER, so water cannot easily separate them","Water molecules repel cellulose"],3,
 "The β-linkages make cellulose chains straight, so neighbouring chains pack closely and form a huge number of interchain hydrogen bonds between their O−H groups. To dissolve, water would have to break all of those at once; collectively they are too strong, so the chains stay bundled in strong fibers. Lots of O−H groups make cellulose hydrophilic (it absorbs water - cotton towels) but not soluble.",
 ["The O−H groups can hydrogen bond with water OR with neighbouring chains.","Straight chains pack closely side by side.","Massive interchain hydrogen bonding."]],
["m11-a-042",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "A nucleotide, the monomer of DNA and RNA, consists of:",
 ["an amino acid, a sugar and a phosphate","a sugar, a phosphate group and a nitrogen-containing base","glucose and a fatty acid","a base pair and two phosphates","glycerol and three fatty acids"],1,
 "Each nucleotide = a five-carbon sugar (deoxyribose in DNA, ribose in RNA) + a phosphate group + a nitrogenous base (A, G, C, and T in DNA or U in RNA). Nucleotides link sugar-to-phosphate into the backbone of each strand, with the bases sticking out.",
 ["Three parts, one of which carries the genetic 'letter'.","The backbone is made of two of the parts.","Sugar + phosphate + base."]],
["m11-a-043",11,23,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "In double-stranded DNA, adenine (A) pairs with ___ and guanine (G) pairs with ___.",
 ["guanine; adenine","adenine; guanine","cytosine; thymine","uracil; cytosine","thymine; cytosine"],4,
 "Complementary base pairing: A pairs with T and G pairs with C, each pair joined by hydrogen bonds (A-T two, G-C three). Uracil replaces thymine only in RNA.",
 ["The pairs are fixed by shape and hydrogen-bonding partners.","The two DNA base pairs are A-T and G-C.","A-T, G-C."]],
["m11-a-044",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "How many hydrogen bonds hold an A-T base pair together, and how many hold a G-C base pair together?",
 ["A-T 3; G-C 2","A-T 2; G-C 3","Both 2","Both 3","A-T 1; G-C 2"],1,
 "An A-T pair is held by two hydrogen bonds and a G-C pair by three. That extra H-bond is why DNA rich in G-C pairs needs a higher temperature to separate its strands (a higher 'melting temperature').",
 ["One pair has one more hydrogen bond than the other.","The G-C pair is the stronger one.","A-T two, G-C three."]],
["m11-a-045",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Four DNA samples of equal length have these G-C contents. Which sample requires the HIGHEST temperature to separate its two strands?",
 ["20% G-C","35% G-C","50% G-C","60% G-C","70% G-C"],4,
 "Each G-C pair contributes three hydrogen bonds, each A-T pair only two, so the higher the G-C fraction, the more hydrogen bonds hold the strands together (G-C pairs also stack more strongly). The 70% G-C sample has the most, so it has the highest melting temperature.",
 ["Which base pair is held together more strongly?","G-C has 3 H-bonds; A-T has 2.","Most G-C = most H-bonds = highest melting temperature."]],
["m11-a-046",11,23,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "A sample of double-stranded DNA is 30% adenine (A). What percentage of its bases are guanine (G)?",
 ["30%","70%","40%","20%","15%"],3,
 "Base pairing means %A = %T and %G = %C. A = 30% → T = 30%, so A + T = 60%, leaving 40% for G + C, split equally: G = 20%, C = 20%. 40% forgets to split G + C; 70% assumes the rest is all G.",
 ["A pairs with T, so %A = %T. Likewise %G = %C.","A + T = 60%. What is left for G + C?","Split the remaining 40% between G and C."]],
["m11-a-047",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which statement about DNA and RNA nucleotides is correct?",
 ["DNA contains ribose and uracil","RNA contains deoxyribose and thymine","DNA contains deoxyribose and thymine; RNA contains ribose and uracil","Both contain the same sugar and the same four bases","DNA nucleotides lack a phosphate group"],2,
 "DNA = deoxyribonucleic acid: its sugar is deoxyribose (one fewer O than ribose) and its bases are A, G, C, T. RNA uses ribose and replaces thymine with uracil (A, G, C, U). Both have phosphate groups in their backbones.",
 ["The 'D' in DNA names the sugar.","One base differs between DNA and RNA.","DNA: deoxyribose + T. RNA: ribose + U."]],
["m11-a-048",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "What holds the two strands of a DNA double helix together?",
 ["Hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs, plus dispersion forces between the stacked bases","Covalent bonds between the two sugar-phosphate backbones","Ionic bonds between the phosphate groups of the two strands","Disulfide bridges","Peptide bonds"],0,
 "Within each strand, covalent bonds link sugar to phosphate. Between the strands, there are no covalent bonds: complementary bases hydrogen bond (A-T 2, G-C 3), and the flat bases stacked up the middle of the helix attract each other through dispersion forces. The phosphates are negatively charged and REPEL each other, which is why they face outward toward the water.",
 ["Within a strand vs between strands - which bonds are where?","The strands must separate easily for copying, so the forces between them are weak.","Base-pair H-bonds plus base-stacking dispersion."]],
["m11-a-049",11,23,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why is it important that the two strands of DNA are held together by many weak hydrogen bonds rather than by covalent bonds?",
 ["Hydrogen bonds are stronger than covalent bonds","Collectively the many H-bonds make the helix stable, but each one is weak enough that the strands can be unzipped locally for replication and transcription at body temperature","Covalent bonds cannot form between nitrogen atoms","Hydrogen bonds make DNA soluble in fats","Weak bonds prevent DNA from ever separating"],1,
 "A gene must be both durable and readable. Millions of base-pair H-bonds (plus stacking) add up to a very stable double helix, yet each individual H-bond (≈ 10-40 kJ/mol) can be broken by enzymes without much energy, so the strands open up a few pairs at a time for copying and close again afterward. Covalent cross-links (≈ 300+ kJ/mol each) would make unzipping far too costly.",
 ["Compare the energy of one hydrogen bond with one covalent bond.","DNA must be stable but also opened for copying.","Weak individually, strong collectively."]],
["m11-a-050",11,23,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "One strand of a DNA segment has the base sequence A T G C C G. How many hydrogen bonds hold this six-base-pair segment to its complementary strand?",
 ["12","14","16","18","6"],2,
 "Pair each base: A-T (2), T-A (2), G-C (3), C-G (3), C-G (3), G-C (3). Total = 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 16. 12 assumes 2 per pair; 18 assumes 3 per pair; 6 counts one per pair.",
 ["Write the complementary base for each position first.","A-T and T-A give 2 each; G-C and C-G give 3 each.","Two A/T pairs and four G/C pairs."]],
["m11-a-051",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "One DNA strand reads A T G C C G (left to right). What is the base sequence of the complementary strand, aligned base-for-base under it?",
 ["A T G C C G","U A C G G C","G C C G T A","T A C G G C","C G T A A T"],3,
 "Replace each base by its partner: A → T, T → A, G → C, C → G, C → G, G → C, giving T A C G G C. Uracil (U) occurs only in RNA. Simply reversing the strand (G C C G T A) is not complementing it.",
 ["Use A-T and G-C pairing at each position.","DNA uses T, not U.","A→T, T→A, G→C, C→G."]],
["m11-a-052",11,23,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which treatment would denature a protein (unfold it) but leave its primary structure intact?",
 ["Boiling it for hours in concentrated hydrochloric acid","Digesting it with an enzyme that cuts peptide bonds","Heating it to about 70 °C in water","Hydrolysing it completely to free amino acids","None - any treatment that unfolds a protein also breaks its peptide bonds"],2,
 "Moderate heating disrupts the weak hydrogen bonds, salt bridges and dispersion interactions of the secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure, so the protein unfolds (like cooking an egg) while the covalent peptide bonds survive. Prolonged boiling in strong acid, protease digestion and complete hydrolysis all BREAK peptide bonds, destroying the primary structure.",
 ["Primary structure = covalent peptide bonds.","Which treatment is too gentle to break covalent bonds but strong enough to break IMFs?","Moderate heat."]],
["m11-a-053",11,23,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which amino acid side chain is MOST likely to be found on the outer surface of a water-soluble protein, in contact with water?",
 ["Phenylalanine, −CH₂C₆H₅","Leucine, −CH₂CH(CH₃)₂","Valine, −CH(CH₃)₂","Alanine, −CH₃","Lysine, −(CH₂)₄NH₃⁺"],4,
 "Lysine's side chain ends in a charged −NH₃⁺ group, which forms strong ion-dipole attractions with water, so it sits on the surface. Phenylalanine, leucine, valine and alanine have nonpolar hydrocarbon side chains, which are tucked into the protein's interior where they interact by dispersion forces.",
 ["Water attracts charged and polar groups.","Which side chain carries a charge?","The −NH₃⁺ group."]],
["m11-a-054",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "All amino acids share a central carbon bonded to an −NH₂ group, a −COOH group and an H atom. What distinguishes one amino acid from another?",
 ["The number of −COOH groups","The number of hydrogen atoms in the −NH₂ group","Whether it contains nitrogen","Whether it contains a peptide bond","The side chain (R group) on the central carbon"],4,
 "The 20 common amino acids differ only in their R group (side chain) - from −H in glycine to −CH₃ in alanine to −CH₂SH in cysteine or −(CH₂)₄NH₃⁺ in lysine. The R groups determine whether a residue is nonpolar, polar or charged, which in turn controls how the protein folds.",
 ["Three of the four groups on the central carbon are the same for all.","What is the fourth group called?","The R group."]],
["m11-a-055",11,23,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement about the DNA double helix is FALSE?",
 ["The sugar-phosphate backbones are on the outside, facing water","The bases are stacked in the interior of the helix","The negatively charged phosphate groups of the two strands attract each other and hold the helix together","A G-C pair is held by three hydrogen bonds","Dispersion forces between stacked bases help stabilize the helix"],2,
 "Phosphate groups each carry a negative charge, so the two backbones REPEL each other; they sit on the outside where water and cations (Na⁺, Mg²⁺) shield the charge. What holds the strands together is base-pair hydrogen bonding plus dispersion forces from base stacking. The other statements are true.",
 ["Do like charges attract or repel?","Where do the phosphate groups sit, and what are their charges?","Negative phosphates repel; base pairs and stacking hold the helix."]],
["m11-a-056",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "When a gas such as O₂ dissolves in water, which statement about entropy and enthalpy is generally true?",
 ["Entropy increases and the process is endothermic","Entropy decreases (the gas molecules lose their freedom) and the process is exothermic","Both entropy and enthalpy increase","Neither changes","Entropy increases and the process is exothermic, so gas solubility rises with temperature"],1,
 "A gas molecule moving freely in the vapor becomes confined among solvent molecules, so the entropy of the gas DEcreases. Dissolving can still happen because it is exothermic: there is no solute-solute step to pay for (the gas molecules are already separated), and solute-solvent attractions release energy. Because it is exothermic, raising T reduces gas solubility.",
 ["Is a gas more or less dispersed after it dissolves?","With no gas-gas attractions to break, what is the sign of ΔH(soln)?","Entropy down, exothermic - so higher T lowers solubility."]],
["m11-a-057",11,23,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which substance would you expect to be MORE soluble in hexane than in water?",
 ["CH₃OH","NaCl","CH₃COOH (acetic acid)","C₈H₁₈ (octane)","Glucose"],3,
 "Octane is a nonpolar hydrocarbon; it interacts with hexane through dispersion forces just as hexane interacts with itself, and the two mix completely, while octane is essentially insoluble in water. Methanol, acetic acid and glucose hydrogen bond with water; NaCl is held in water by ion-dipole forces - all far more soluble in water than in hexane.",
 ["Which solute is nonpolar?","Like dissolves like.","A hydrocarbon dissolves in a hydrocarbon."]],
["m11-a-058",11,23,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "For an ionic solid, ΔH(soln) = ΔH(lattice) + ΔH(hydration). For a certain salt, ΔH(lattice) = +700 kJ/mol and ΔH(hydration) = −720 kJ/mol. Which statement is correct?",
 ["The salt dissolves endothermically and the solution warms up","The salt dissolves exothermically (ΔH(soln) = −20 kJ/mol) and the solution warms up","The salt dissolves exothermically and the solution cools down","ΔH(soln) = +1420 kJ/mol","The salt cannot dissolve because the lattice energy is so large"],1,
 "ΔH(soln) = +700 + (−720) = −20 kJ/mol. Negative ΔH(soln) means heat is released to the surroundings - the water - so the solution warms. A large lattice energy is not a barrier by itself; what matters is how it compares with the hydration energy. +1420 adds magnitudes; 'exothermic but cools' mixes up which way the heat flows.",
 ["Add the two ΔH values with their signs.","Negative ΔH means heat flows from the system into the water.","−20 kJ/mol, and the water gets warmer."]],
["m11-a-059",11,23,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why does a lake in summer often hold less dissolved oxygen than the same lake in winter?",
 ["Warm water is more polar","Gas solubility decreases as temperature increases","Warm water has a higher O₂ partial pressure above it","O₂ reacts with warm water","Solubility of all substances decreases with temperature"],1,
 "Dissolving O₂ is exothermic, so raising the temperature shifts it toward the gas escaping; warmer water holds less O₂ at the same partial pressure (about 14.6 mg/L at 0 °C vs 8.3 mg/L at 25 °C). The statement that ALL substances become less soluble is false - most solids become more soluble in hotter water.",
 ["How does temperature affect gas solubility?","Compare a cold soda and a warm one.","Higher T, lower gas solubility."]],
["m11-a-060",11,23,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which kind of intermolecular force is mainly responsible for the solubility of NH₃ gas in water (about 30% by mass)?",
 ["Hydrogen bonding between NH₃ and H₂O","Ion-dipole forces","Dispersion forces","Dipole-induced dipole forces","Ion-induced dipole forces"],0,
 "NH₃ has N−H bonds and a lone pair on N, and water has O−H bonds and lone pairs on O, so they hydrogen bond in both directions (water's H to NH₃'s N, and NH₃'s H to water's O). That strong solute-solvent attraction makes ammonia extremely water-soluble compared with nonpolar gases like N₂ or O₂, which rely on dipole-induced dipole forces.",
 ["Does NH₃ have an H on N, O or F? A lone pair on N?","Water can both donate and accept hydrogen bonds.","NH₃ and H₂O hydrogen bond with each other."]],
]);

/* ---------------- Gym 11 · Lesson 24: concentration units and colligative properties ---------------- */
chemAddQuestions([
["m11-a-061",11,24,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Molality (m) is defined as:",
 ["moles of solute per liter of solution","moles of solute per kilogram of solution","moles of solute per kilogram of solvent","grams of solute per liter of solvent","moles of solute per mole of solution"],2,
 "Molality = mol solute ÷ kg SOLVENT. Molarity uses liters of SOLUTION; mole fraction uses total moles. Because it is built from masses, molality does not change when the solution warms and expands, which is why the boiling-point and freezing-point formulas use it.",
 ["Molality is mass-based, not volume-based.","Is the denominator the solvent or the whole solution?","mol solute / kg solvent."]],
["m11-a-062",11,24,2,"Calculation","num",
 "10.0 g of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180.16 g/mol) is dissolved in 250. g of water. What is the molality of the solution? (Enter a number in mol/kg.)",
 ["0.222 m"],0.2220,
 "mol glucose = 10.0 g ÷ 180.16 g/mol = 0.05551 mol. kg of solvent = 0.250 kg. m = 0.05551 ÷ 0.250 = 0.222 mol/kg. Dividing by the mass of the whole solution (0.260 kg) gives 0.213 m - molality uses the solvent only.",
 ["Molality = mol solute ÷ kg solvent.","Convert 10.0 g glucose to moles and 250. g water to kg.","0.05551 mol ÷ 0.250 kg."]],
["m11-a-063",11,24,2,"Calculation","mcq",
 "For the same solution (10.0 g glucose, 180.16 g/mol, in 250. g water), what is the mole fraction of glucose?",
 ["0.0555","0.222","3.85 × 10⁻²","3.99 × 10⁻³","0.996"],3,
 "n(glucose) = 0.05551 mol; n(water) = 250. ÷ 18.02 = 13.87 mol. X(glucose) = 0.05551 ÷ (0.05551 + 13.87) = 3.99 × 10⁻³. 0.0555 is the moles of glucose, 0.222 the molality, 3.85 × 10⁻² the mass fraction, and 0.996 the mole fraction of WATER.",
 ["Mole fraction = moles of glucose ÷ total moles.","Convert both components to moles.","0.05551 ÷ (0.05551 + 13.87)."]],
["m11-a-064",11,24,2,"Calculation","mcq",
 "What is the mass percent of glucose in a solution of 10.0 g glucose in 250. g water?",
 ["4.00%","3.85%","0.222%","0.399%","96.2%"],1,
 "Mass percent = mass solute ÷ mass of SOLUTION × 100 = 10.0 ÷ (10.0 + 250.) × 100 = 3.85%. 4.00% divides by the solvent mass only; 0.399% is the mole fraction × 100; 96.2% is the water's mass percent.",
 ["The denominator is the whole solution.","Solution mass = solute + solvent.","10.0 ÷ 260. × 100."]],
["m11-a-065",11,24,4,"Calculation","num",
 "A 1.50 M aqueous NaCl solution has a density of 1.06 g/mL. What is its molality? (Enter a number in mol/kg.)",
 ["1.54 m"],1.543,
 "Take exactly 1.000 L of solution. Mass of solution = 1000 mL × 1.06 g/mL = 1060 g. It contains 1.50 mol NaCl = 1.50 × 58.44 = 87.66 g. Mass of water = 1060 − 87.66 = 972.3 g = 0.9723 kg. m = 1.50 mol ÷ 0.9723 kg = 1.54 m. The trap is dividing by the mass of the SOLUTION (1.50/1.060 = 1.42) without subtracting the solute.",
 ["Pick a convenient amount: 1.000 L of solution.","Use density for the solution mass, then subtract the NaCl mass to get the water mass.","1.50 mol ÷ (1.060 − 0.08766) kg."]],
["m11-a-066",11,24,4,"Calculation","mcq",
 "A hydrogen peroxide solution is 30.0% H₂O₂ by mass and has a density of 1.11 g/mL. What is its molality?",
 ["8.82 m","9.79 m","0.185 m","30.0 m","12.6 m"],4,
 "Take 100.0 g of solution: 30.0 g H₂O₂ and 70.0 g water. n(H₂O₂) = 30.0 ÷ 34.02 = 0.8819 mol. m = 0.8819 mol ÷ 0.0700 kg = 12.6 m. The density is needed only for molarity: V = 100.0/1.11 = 90.1 mL, so M = 9.79 - a distractor. 8.82 m divides by the 0.100 kg of solution; 0.185 is the mole fraction of H₂O₂.",
 ["Assume 100.0 g of solution so the mass percent becomes grams.","Molality needs kg of WATER: 100.0 − 30.0 g.","0.8819 mol ÷ 0.0700 kg."]],
["m11-a-067",11,24,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why are boiling-point elevation and freezing-point depression calculated with molality rather than molarity?",
 ["Molality is easier to measure with a buret","Molality is based on masses, so it does not change with temperature, whereas a solution's volume (and molarity) changes as it is heated or cooled","Molarity is only for gases","Molality counts the ions; molarity does not","Molality is always larger than molarity"],1,
 "These experiments change the temperature, and liquids expand when heated, so the liters in molarity change while the moles do not. Masses do not change with temperature, so molality stays constant throughout. (For dilute aqueous solutions the two are nearly equal anyway, since 1 L ≈ 1 kg of water.)",
 ["What happens to the volume of a liquid when it is heated?","Which unit uses only masses?","Mass does not depend on temperature."]],
["m11-a-068",11,24,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the ideal van 't Hoff factor (i) for CaCl₂ dissolved in water?",
 ["1","2","3","4","5"],2,
 "CaCl₂(s) → Ca²⁺(aq) + 2 Cl⁻(aq): one formula unit gives three particles, so i = 3. Counting only 'cation + anion' as 2 forgets the subscript on Cl.",
 ["Write the dissociation equation.","Count every ion released per formula unit.","1 Ca²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻."]],
["m11-a-069",11,24,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which solute has an ideal van 't Hoff factor of 5?",
 ["Glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆","NaCl","MgCl₂","(NH₄)₃PO₄","Al₂(SO₄)₃"],4,
 "Al₂(SO₄)₃ → 2 Al³⁺ + 3 SO₄²⁻ = 5 particles. (NH₄)₃PO₄ → 3 NH₄⁺ + PO₄³⁻ = 4; MgCl₂ = 3; NaCl = 2; glucose is a nonelectrolyte, i = 1. Polyatomic ions stay intact - SO₄²⁻ is one particle, not five atoms.",
 ["Polyatomic ions such as SO₄²⁻ stay together as one particle.","Write each dissociation and count ions.","2 Al³⁺ + 3 SO₄²⁻."]],
["m11-a-070",11,24,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which solute has a van 't Hoff factor closest to 1 in water?",
 ["KBr","Ethylene glycol, HOCH₂CH₂OH","Na₂SO₄","HCl","CaCl₂"],1,
 "Ethylene glycol is a molecular nonelectrolyte: it dissolves as intact molecules, so i = 1. KBr (2), HCl (a strong acid, 2), Na₂SO₄ (3) and CaCl₂ (3) all break into ions. A weak electrolyte such as acetic acid would sit only slightly above 1.",
 ["Which solute does not break into ions?","Alcohols and sugars are nonelectrolytes.","The glycol."]],
["m11-a-071",11,24,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "A 0.100 m aqueous MgSO₄ solution freezes at −0.225 °C. K(f) for water = 1.86 °C/m. What is the measured van 't Hoff factor, and why does it differ from the ideal value?",
 ["i = 2.00; MgSO₄ dissociates completely","i = 1.21; ion pairing - some Mg²⁺ and SO₄²⁻ stay associated, so fewer independent particles form","i = 1.21; MgSO₄ is a nonelectrolyte","i = 0.83; MgSO₄ only partly dissolves","i = 2.42; each ion pair counts twice"],1,
 "i = ΔT(f) ÷ (K(f)·m) = 0.225 ÷ (1.86 × 0.100) = 1.21, far below the ideal 2. In solution, oppositely charged ions attract and some travel together as ion pairs, which count as one particle. Ion pairing is strongest for highly charged ions (Mg²⁺ and SO₄²⁻) and in more concentrated solutions. MgSO₄ is a strong electrolyte, not a nonelectrolyte.",
 ["Rearrange ΔT(f) = i·K(f)·m for i.","0.225 ÷ (1.86 × 0.100).","Fewer particles than ideal → ion pairing."]],
["m11-a-072",11,24,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Measured van 't Hoff factors for strong electrolytes are usually SMALLER than the ideal values. Which statement is correct?",
 ["The deviation is largest for dilute solutions of singly charged ions","The deviation is due to ion pairing and is greater for more concentrated solutions and for ions with higher charges","The deviation means strong electrolytes do not dissociate at all","Measured i is always larger than ideal","The deviation is caused by evaporation of the solvent"],1,
 "Oppositely charged ions attract; in solution some cluster as ion pairs, lowering the number of independent particles. The closer the ions are (higher concentration) and the larger their charges, the more pairing occurs: 0.05 m NaCl has i ≈ 1.9, while 0.1 m MgSO₄ has i ≈ 1.2. Very dilute solutions approach the ideal value.",
 ["What do oppositely charged ions do when they meet in solution?","More crowding and more charge = more pairing.","Ion pairing grows with concentration and ionic charge."]],
["m11-a-073",11,24,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Raoult's law for a solution of a nonvolatile solute states that:",
 ["P(solution) = X(solute) · P°(solvent)","P(solution) = X(solvent) · P°(solvent)","P(solution) = P°(solvent) + X(solute)","P(solution) = k(H) · X(solvent)","P(solution) = i·K·m"],1,
 "The vapor above the solution comes only from the solvent, and its pressure is proportional to the solvent's mole fraction: P = X(solvent)·P°(solvent). Since X(solvent) < 1, the vapor pressure is lowered; the lowering itself is ΔP = X(solute)·P°(solvent). Using X(solute) in place of X(solvent) gives the lowering, not the vapor pressure.",
 ["Only the solvent evaporates if the solute is nonvolatile.","The vapor pressure scales with how much of the liquid is solvent.","X(solvent) × P°(solvent)."]],
["m11-a-074",11,24,3,"Calculation","num",
 "50.0 g of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180.16 g/mol) is dissolved in 500. g of water at 25 °C, where pure water has a vapor pressure of 23.76 torr. By how much is the vapor pressure LOWERED? (Enter a number in torr.)",
 ["0.235 torr"],0.2353,
 "n(glucose) = 50.0 ÷ 180.16 = 0.2775 mol; n(water) = 500. ÷ 18.02 = 27.75 mol. X(water) = 27.75 ÷ (27.75 + 0.2775) = 0.99010. P(solution) = 0.99010 × 23.76 = 23.52 torr. Lowering ΔP = 23.76 − 23.52 = 0.235 torr (equivalently X(glucose) × P° = 0.00990 × 23.76). The answer is the small DIFFERENCE, not the new vapor pressure.",
 ["Find the mole fractions of water and glucose.","ΔP = X(solute) × P°(solvent).","0.00990 × 23.76 torr."],{tol:0.02}],
["m11-a-075",11,24,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "10.0 g of NaCl is dissolved in 100. g of water at 25 °C (P°(water) = 23.76 torr). Assuming complete dissociation, what is the vapor pressure of the solution?",
 ["23.76 torr","23.05 torr","1.38 torr","22.38 torr","25.14 torr"],3,
 "n(NaCl) = 10.0 ÷ 58.44 = 0.1711 mol → 0.3422 mol of ions (i = 2). n(water) = 100. ÷ 18.02 = 5.549 mol. X(water) = 5.549 ÷ (5.549 + 0.3422) = 0.9419. P = 0.9419 × 23.76 = 22.38 torr. 23.05 torr ignores dissociation (i = 1); 23.76 torr is pure water; 1.38 torr is the LOWERING, not the vapor pressure; 25.14 torr adds the lowering instead of subtracting it.",
 ["Vapor-pressure lowering counts particles, so count ions.","NaCl gives 2 moles of ions per mole.","X(water) = 5.549 ÷ (5.549 + 0.3422); multiply by 23.76."]],
["m11-a-076",11,24,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why does dissolving a nonvolatile solute lower the vapor pressure of a solvent?",
 ["The solute reacts with the vapor","Solute particles take up some of the surface and lower the solvent's mole fraction, so fewer solvent molecules escape per unit time; the solution is also more disordered, so the solvent has less tendency to leave","The solute raises the temperature of the solvent","The solute increases the strength of every solvent-solvent attraction","The solute evaporates along with the solvent"],1,
 "Two complementary pictures: (1) fewer solvent molecules at the surface means fewer escape per second, so the equilibrium vapor pressure is lower; (2) the solution already has higher entropy than pure solvent, so there is less entropy gain from vaporizing. Either way, P = X(solvent)·P°. A nonvolatile solute contributes no vapor of its own.",
 ["What fraction of the surface is solvent now?","Think about how many solvent molecules can escape per second.","Lower solvent mole fraction → lower vapor pressure."]],
["m11-a-077",11,24,2,"Calculation","mcq",
 "Assuming ideal behavior, what is the freezing point of 0.150 m aqueous CaCl₂? K(f) = 1.86 °C/m.",
 ["−0.279 °C","−0.558 °C","+0.837 °C","−0.837 °C","−0.242 °C"],3,
 "i = 3 for CaCl₂. ΔT(f) = i·K(f)·m = 3 × 1.86 × 0.150 = 0.837 °C, and the freezing point goes DOWN: 0.000 − 0.837 = −0.837 °C. −0.279 °C uses i = 1; −0.558 °C uses i = 2; +0.837 °C raises the freezing point; −0.242 °C divides by K(f) instead of multiplying.",
 ["CaCl₂ → Ca²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻. What is i?","ΔT(f) = i × K(f) × m.","3 × 1.86 × 0.150, subtracted from 0 °C."]],
["m11-a-078",11,24,3,"Calculation","num",
 "An antifreeze solution is made from 1.00 kg of ethylene glycol (C₂H₆O₂, 62.07 g/mol) in 4.00 kg of water. What is its freezing point? K(f) = 1.86 °C/m. (Enter a number in °C.)",
 ["−7.49 °C"],-7.492,
 "n = 1000 g ÷ 62.07 g/mol = 16.11 mol. m = 16.11 mol ÷ 4.00 kg = 4.028 m. Ethylene glycol is a nonelectrolyte (i = 1). ΔT(f) = 1.86 × 4.028 = 7.49 °C, so the freezing point is 0.00 − 7.49 = −7.49 °C. Using the total 5.00 kg as the 'solvent' gives −5.99 °C.",
 ["Molality uses the kg of WATER only.","Ethylene glycol does not ionize: i = 1.","m = (1000/62.07)/4.00; ΔT(f) = 1.86 × m."]],
["m11-a-079",11,24,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "What is the boiling point of the same antifreeze solution (1.00 kg ethylene glycol, 62.07 g/mol, in 4.00 kg water)? K(b) = 0.512 °C/m.",
 ["2.06 °C","101.65 °C","102.06 °C","104.12 °C","97.94 °C"],2,
 "m = 4.028 mol/kg (from 16.11 mol in 4.00 kg water). ΔT(b) = K(b)·m = 0.512 × 4.028 = 2.06 °C, and the boiling point RISES: 100.00 + 2.06 = 102.06 °C. 2.06 °C is ΔT alone; 101.65 °C uses 5.00 kg; 104.12 °C uses i = 2; 97.94 °C lowers the boiling point. The same solution both freezes lower and boils higher - antifreeze is also 'anti-boil'.",
 ["Same molality as for the freezing point; now use K(b).","ΔT(b) = K(b)·m, added to 100.00 °C.","0.512 × 4.028 = 2.06."]],
["m11-a-080",11,24,2,"Calculation","num",
 "What is the freezing point of a solution made by dissolving 15.0 g of urea, CO(NH₂)₂ (60.06 g/mol, a nonelectrolyte), in 250. g of water? K(f) = 1.86 °C/m. (Enter a number in °C.)",
 ["−1.86 °C"],-1.858,
 "n = 15.0 ÷ 60.06 = 0.2498 mol. m = 0.2498 ÷ 0.250 kg = 0.999 m. ΔT(f) = 1 × 1.86 × 0.999 = 1.86 °C. Freezing point = −1.86 °C. Urea is molecular, so i = 1; treating it as an electrolyte (i = 2) gives −3.72 °C.",
 ["Find the molality first: mol urea ÷ kg water.","Urea does not ionize, so i = 1.","ΔT(f) = 1.86 × 0.999."]],
["m11-a-081",11,24,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "5.85 g of NaCl is dissolved in 250. g of water. Assuming complete dissociation, what is the boiling point of the solution? K(b) = 0.512 °C/m.",
 ["100.20 °C","100.41 °C","101.49 °C","99.59 °C","100.82 °C"],1,
 "n(NaCl) = 5.85 ÷ 58.44 = 0.1001 mol; m = 0.1001 ÷ 0.250 = 0.4004 m. i = 2. ΔT(b) = 2 × 0.512 × 0.4004 = 0.410 °C. Boiling point = 100.41 °C. 100.20 °C ignores dissociation; 101.49 °C uses K(f) instead of K(b); 99.59 °C subtracts; 100.82 °C uses i = 4.",
 ["Molality, then i, then K(b).","NaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻, i = 2.","2 × 0.512 × 0.4004 °C added to 100.00 °C."]],
["m11-a-082",11,24,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Assuming ideal behavior, which aqueous solution has the LOWEST freezing point?",
 ["0.15 m NaCl","0.12 m MgCl₂","0.20 m glucose","0.10 m KBr","0.10 m Na₃PO₄"],4,
 "Freezing-point depression ∝ i·m (total particle concentration). NaCl: 2 × 0.15 = 0.30. MgCl₂: 3 × 0.12 = 0.36. Glucose: 1 × 0.20 = 0.20. KBr: 2 × 0.10 = 0.20. Na₃PO₄ (3 Na⁺ + PO₄³⁻): 4 × 0.10 = 0.40 - the largest, so the biggest ΔT(f) and the lowest freezing point. Picking the largest molality (glucose) ignores i.",
 ["Compute i × m for each solution.","Na₃PO₄ gives 4 ions; MgCl₂ gives 3.","Largest i·m = lowest freezing point."]],
["m11-a-083",11,24,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Rank these aqueous solutions in order of INCREASING freezing point (lowest freezing point first), assuming ideal behavior: 0.050 m Na₂SO₄, 0.10 m sucrose, 0.040 m NaCl, 0.030 m AlCl₃.",
 ["Na₂SO₄ < AlCl₃ < sucrose < NaCl","NaCl < sucrose < AlCl₃ < Na₂SO₄","Sucrose < Na₂SO₄ < NaCl < AlCl₃","AlCl₃ < Na₂SO₄ < NaCl < sucrose","Na₂SO₄ < sucrose < AlCl₃ < NaCl"],0,
 "Particle concentrations i·m: Na₂SO₄ 3 × 0.050 = 0.15; AlCl₃ 4 × 0.030 = 0.12; sucrose 1 × 0.10 = 0.10; NaCl 2 × 0.040 = 0.08. The largest i·m is depressed the most and freezes LOWEST, so increasing freezing point: Na₂SO₄ (−0.279 °C) < AlCl₃ (−0.223 °C) < sucrose (−0.186 °C) < NaCl (−0.149 °C). Ranking by molality alone puts sucrose first.",
 ["Multiply each molality by its i.","Bigger i·m means a LOWER freezing point.","0.15, 0.12, 0.10, 0.08 - lowest freezing point first."]],
["m11-a-084",11,24,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Per mole of salt spread on an icy road, why is CaCl₂ more effective at melting ice than NaCl?",
 ["CaCl₂ has a higher molar mass","CaCl₂ produces 3 moles of ions per mole versus 2 for NaCl, so it lowers the freezing point more","CaCl₂ is a nonelectrolyte","NaCl raises the freezing point of water","CaCl₂ reacts with ice to form new compounds"],1,
 "Freezing-point depression depends on the number of dissolved particles. Per mole, CaCl₂ → Ca²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻ (i = 3), NaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻ (i = 2), so CaCl₂ depresses the freezing point about 1.5 times as much. (Its exothermic dissolving also helps melt ice.) Molar mass does not matter for a per-mole comparison.",
 ["Colligative properties count particles.","Compare i for each salt.","3 particles beat 2."]],
["m11-a-085",11,24,4,"Calculation","num",
 "How many grams of NaCl must be dissolved in 1.00 kg of water to lower its freezing point to −5.00 °C? Assume i = 2 and K(f) = 1.86 °C/m. (Enter a number in g.)",
 ["78.5 g"],78.55,
 "ΔT(f) = 5.00 °C = i·K(f)·m, so m = 5.00 ÷ (2 × 1.86) = 1.344 mol/kg. In 1.00 kg of water that is 1.344 mol NaCl × 58.44 g/mol = 78.5 g. Forgetting i doubles the answer to 157 g.",
 ["Work backwards from ΔT(f) to molality.","m = ΔT(f) ÷ (i × K(f)).","1.344 mol × 58.44 g/mol."]],
["m11-a-086",11,24,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "1.00 g of an unknown nonelectrolyte is dissolved in 20.0 g of benzene. The freezing point drops by 1.35 °C. K(f) for benzene = 5.12 °C/m. What is the molar mass of the unknown?",
 ["0.190 g/mol","68.9 g/mol","5.27 g/mol","379 g/mol","190. g/mol"],4,
 "m = ΔT(f) ÷ K(f) = 1.35 ÷ 5.12 = 0.2637 mol/kg. Moles of solute = 0.2637 mol/kg × 0.0200 kg = 5.273 × 10⁻³ mol. Molar mass = 1.00 g ÷ 5.273 × 10⁻³ mol = 190. g/mol. 0.190 g/mol uses 20.0 kg instead of 0.0200 kg; 68.9 g/mol uses water's K(f); 379 g/mol wrongly uses i = 2 for a nonelectrolyte; 5.27 reports the millimoles.",
 ["ΔT(f) → molality → moles → molar mass.","Use benzene's K(f) and the solvent mass in kg.","1.00 g ÷ (0.2637 × 0.0200) mol."]],
["m11-a-087",11,24,4,"Calculation","num",
 "0.100 g of a nonelectrolyte dissolved in 2.00 g of camphor lowers camphor's freezing point by 11.3 °C. K(f) for camphor = 37.7 °C/m. Calculate the molar mass of the solute. (Enter a number in g/mol.)",
 ["167 g/mol"],166.8,
 "m = 11.3 ÷ 37.7 = 0.2997 mol/kg. Moles = 0.2997 × 0.00200 kg = 5.995 × 10⁻⁴ mol. Molar mass = 0.100 g ÷ 5.995 × 10⁻⁴ mol = 167 g/mol. Camphor's huge K(f) is why it is used for molar-mass determinations: a tiny sample gives a large, easily measured ΔT. Using water's K(f) gives 8.23 g/mol.",
 ["Same chain: ΔT → m → mol → g/mol.","2.00 g of camphor is 0.00200 kg.","0.100 g ÷ (11.3/37.7 × 0.00200) mol."]],
["m11-a-088",11,24,3,"Calculation","num",
 "2.50 g of a nonvolatile nonelectrolyte dissolved in 50.0 g of water raises the boiling point by 0.284 °C. K(b) = 0.512 °C/m. Calculate the molar mass of the solute. (Enter a number in g/mol.)",
 ["90.1 g/mol"],90.14,
 "m = ΔT(b) ÷ K(b) = 0.284 ÷ 0.512 = 0.5547 mol/kg. Moles = 0.5547 × 0.0500 kg = 0.02773 mol. Molar mass = 2.50 g ÷ 0.02773 mol = 90.1 g/mol. Using K(f) instead of K(b) gives 327 g/mol.",
 ["A boiling-point problem uses K(b).","m = 0.284/0.512; multiply by 0.0500 kg for moles.","2.50 g ÷ 0.02773 mol."]],
["m11-a-089",11,24,4,"Calculation","mcq",
 "A 0.050 m aqueous solution of an unknown strong electrolyte freezes at −0.279 °C. K(f) = 1.86 °C/m. Which compound could it be?",
 ["Glucose","NaCl","CaCl₂","AlCl₃","Na₃PO₄"],2,
 "i = ΔT(f) ÷ (K(f)·m) = 0.279 ÷ (1.86 × 0.050) = 3.0, so each formula unit releases three ions. CaCl₂ → Ca²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻ fits. Glucose (1), NaCl (2), AlCl₃ (4) and Na₃PO₄ (4) do not.",
 ["Solve ΔT(f) = i·K(f)·m for i.","0.279 ÷ 0.093.","Which compound gives three ions per formula unit?"]],
["m11-a-090",11,24,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "During osmosis across a semipermeable membrane, solvent flows:",
 ["from the more concentrated solution to the more dilute one","from the more dilute solution (lower solute concentration) to the more concentrated one","in whichever direction the solute flows","only when both solutions have the same concentration","from higher to lower osmotic pressure"],1,
 "The membrane lets solvent through but not solute. Solvent moves from where its own concentration is higher - the dilute side - into the concentrated side, tending to even out the concentrations. Osmotic pressure is the pressure needed to stop that flow; it is higher for the MORE concentrated solution, which is where the solvent goes.",
 ["Only the solvent can cross.","Water moves to dilute the side with more solute.","Dilute → concentrated."]],
["m11-a-091",11,24,2,"Calculation","mcq",
 "What is the osmotic pressure of 0.100 M NaCl at 25 °C, assuming complete dissociation? R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K).",
 ["4.89 atm","0.410 atm","2.45 atm","9.79 atm","0.205 atm"],0,
 "Π = i·M·R·T = 2 × 0.100 mol/L × 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K) × 298.15 K = 4.89 atm. 2.45 atm leaves out i; 0.410 atm uses 25 instead of 298.15 K; 9.79 atm uses i = 4; 0.205 atm makes both mistakes (no i, °C). Even a dilute salt solution has a sizable osmotic pressure - almost 5 atm.",
 ["Π = iMRT with T in kelvin.","NaCl: i = 2.","2 × 0.100 × 0.08206 × 298.15."]],
["m11-a-092",11,24,3,"Calculation","num",
 "What is the osmotic pressure of a 0.0100 M glucose solution at body temperature, 37 °C? R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K). (Enter a number in atm.)",
 ["0.255 atm"],0.2545,
 "Glucose is a nonelectrolyte, i = 1. T = 37 + 273.15 = 310.15 K. Π = 1 × 0.0100 × 0.08206 × 310.15 = 0.255 atm. Using 37 instead of 310.15 gives 0.0304 atm; using R = 8.314 gives 25.8 (wrong units).",
 ["Π = iMRT; glucose has i = 1.","Convert 37 °C to kelvin.","0.0100 × 0.08206 × 310.15."]],
["m11-a-093",11,24,4,"Exam","num",
 "A solution of 25.0 mg of a protein in enough water to make 5.00 mL of solution has an osmotic pressure of 3.85 torr at 25 °C. Calculate the molar mass of the protein. R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K). (Enter a number in g/mol.)",
 ["2.41 × 10⁴ g/mol"],24150,
 "Π = 3.85 torr ÷ 760 = 5.066 × 10⁻³ atm. M = Π/(RT) = 5.066 × 10⁻³ ÷ (0.08206 × 298.15) = 2.071 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L. Moles = 2.071 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L × 0.00500 L = 1.035 × 10⁻⁶ mol. Molar mass = 0.0250 g ÷ 1.035 × 10⁻⁶ mol = 2.41 × 10⁴ g/mol. Traps: leaving Π in torr (gives about 32 g/mol), mg not converted to g, or °C for T.",
 ["Convert Π to atm and T to kelvin first.","M = Π/(RT); moles = M × 0.00500 L.","0.0250 g ÷ 1.035 × 10⁻⁶ mol."],{tol:0.02}],
["m11-a-094",11,24,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "Intravenous saline is 0.150 M NaCl. What molarity of glucose would be isotonic with it (same osmotic pressure at the same temperature)?",
 ["0.075 M","0.150 M","0.300 M","0.450 M","0.600 M"],2,
 "Isotonic means equal Π = iMRT, so equal i·M. NaCl: 2 × 0.150 = 0.300 M of particles. Glucose (i = 1) must therefore be 0.300 M. (At 37 °C both have Π ≈ 7.64 atm.) 0.150 M matches molarity but ignores that NaCl gives two particles per formula unit.",
 ["Isotonic = equal osmotic pressure = equal particle concentration.","NaCl particles: 2 × 0.150 M.","Glucose has i = 1, so it must supply 0.300 M on its own."]],
["m11-a-095",11,24,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "A red blood cell is placed in pure water. What happens, and why?",
 ["It shrinks, because water flows out of the cell","Nothing, because pure water is isotonic","It swells and may burst, because water flows into the cell, where the solute concentration is higher","It shrinks, because solute flows into the water","It swells because solute flows into the cell"],2,
 "Pure water is hypotonic compared with the cell's interior (≈ 0.3 M particles). Water crosses the membrane from the dilute side (outside) to the concentrated side (inside), so the cell swells and can burst (hemolysis). In a concentrated (hypertonic) salt solution the opposite happens and the cell shrivels (crenation). The membrane blocks the solutes; only water moves.",
 ["Which side has the higher solute concentration?","Water moves toward the higher solute concentration.","Water rushes in; the cell swells."]],
["m11-a-096",11,24,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why is osmotic pressure, rather than freezing-point depression, used to find the molar mass of a large protein?",
 ["Proteins do not dissolve in water","For the tiny molar concentrations of a protein solution, Π is large enough to measure accurately, while ΔT(f) would be only a few ten-thousandths of a degree","Osmotic pressure does not depend on the number of particles","Freezing destroys the primary structure of the protein","Proteins raise the freezing point instead of lowering it"],1,
 "A few mg of a 24 000 g/mol protein in a few mL is about 2 × 10⁻⁴ M. That gives Π ≈ 0.005 atm ≈ 3.9 torr - easily measured as a column of liquid - but ΔT(f) ≈ 1.86 × 2 × 10⁻⁴ ≈ 0.0004 °C, too small to measure reliably. Both are colligative and both count particles; the difference is sensitivity.",
 ["Estimate the molar concentration of a protein solution.","Compare iMRT with i·K(f)·m for about 2 × 10⁻⁴ M.","Millimeters of liquid vs ten-thousandths of a degree."]],
["m11-a-097",11,24,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement about colligative properties is FALSE?",
 ["0.10 m glucose and 0.10 m sucrose freeze at the same temperature","A 0.10 m NaCl solution freezes at the same temperature as 0.10 m glucose","Adding a nonvolatile solute raises the boiling point of the solvent","Osmotic pressure depends on the concentration of solute particles","A solution of a nonvolatile solute has a lower vapor pressure than the pure solvent"],1,
 "NaCl gives two particles per formula unit (i = 2), so 0.10 m NaCl depresses the freezing point about twice as much as 0.10 m glucose (−0.372 °C vs −0.186 °C). Glucose and sucrose are both nonelectrolytes, so at equal molality they behave the same regardless of their different molar masses - that is what 'colligative' means.",
 ["Colligative properties depend on the NUMBER of particles.","Which solute splits into ions?","NaCl makes twice as many particles as glucose."]],
["m11-a-098",11,24,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which of these is NOT a colligative property?",
 ["Vapor-pressure lowering","Boiling-point elevation","Freezing-point depression","Osmotic pressure","Density of the solution"],4,
 "The four colligative properties - vapor-pressure lowering, boiling-point elevation, freezing-point depression and osmotic pressure - depend only on the number of solute particles. Density depends on WHICH solute is dissolved (its molar mass and how it packs), so it is not colligative.",
 ["Colligative = depends only on the number of particles.","Which property would differ for equal moles of a light vs a heavy solute?","Density."]],
["m11-a-099",11,24,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "What is the mole fraction of glucose in a 1.00 m aqueous glucose solution?",
 ["1.00","0.0180","0.0177","0.982","0.0555"],2,
 "1.00 m means 1.00 mol glucose per 1.000 kg water. n(water) = 1000 ÷ 18.02 = 55.49 mol. X(glucose) = 1.00 ÷ (1.00 + 55.49) = 0.0177. 0.0180 divides by water's moles only (forgetting to include the glucose in the total); 0.982 is X(water); 0.0555 is 1/18.02.",
 ["Molality fixes the moles of solute per 1 kg of water.","Convert 1000 g of water to moles.","1.00 ÷ (1.00 + 55.49)."]],
["m11-a-100",11,24,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why does a nonvolatile solute RAISE the boiling point of water?",
 ["The solute releases heat when it dissolves","The solute lowers the vapor pressure of the water, so the solution must be heated to a higher temperature before its vapor pressure reaches the external pressure","The solute strengthens the covalent O−H bonds","The solute evaporates first and must be removed","The solute increases the external pressure"],1,
 "A liquid boils when its vapor pressure equals the external pressure. Dissolving a nonvolatile solute lowers the solvent's vapor pressure at every temperature (Raoult's law), so the solution's vapor-pressure curve sits below that of pure water and crosses 1 atm at a higher temperature: ΔT(b) = i·K(b)·m. The same lowering shifts the freezing point down.",
 ["Connect boiling to vapor pressure.","What does the solute do to the vapor pressure at 100 °C?","Lower vapor pressure → must heat further to reach 1 atm."]],
["m11-a-101",11,24,4,"Calculation","mcq",
 "Seawater is roughly 3.5% NaCl by mass. Treating it as an ideal NaCl solution (i = 2), estimate its freezing point. K(f) = 1.86 °C/m.",
 ["−1.15 °C","−2.23 °C","−2.31 °C","+2.31 °C","−0.130 °C"],2,
 "Take 100.0 g of seawater: 3.5 g NaCl and 96.5 g water. n(NaCl) = 3.5 ÷ 58.44 = 0.0599 mol; m = 0.0599 ÷ 0.0965 kg = 0.621 m. ΔT(f) = 2 × 1.86 × 0.621 = 2.31 °C, so the freezing point is about −2.31 °C (real seawater freezes near −1.9 °C, because of ion pairing and other salts). −1.15 °C ignores i; −2.23 °C divides by the 0.100 kg of SOLUTION instead of the water; −0.130 °C treats 3.5% as if it were 0.035 m.",
 ["Assume 100.0 g of seawater.","Molality needs the kg of water: 96.5 g.","2 × 1.86 × (3.5/58.44)/0.0965."]],
["m11-a-102",11,24,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "The constants K(b) and K(f) in ΔT(b) = i·K(b)·m and ΔT(f) = i·K(f)·m depend on:",
 ["the solute only","the solvent only","both the solute and the solvent","the external pressure only","the amount of solution"],1,
 "K(b) and K(f) are properties of the SOLVENT (water: 0.512 and 1.86 °C/m; benzene: 2.53 and 5.12 °C/m; camphor: K(f) = 37.7 °C/m). The solute enters only through i and m - its identity does not matter, which is exactly what makes these properties colligative.",
 ["Colligative properties do not care what the solute is.","Where does the identity of the solvent enter the equation?","Solvent only."]],
["m11-a-103",11,24,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "For a dilute aqueous solution, molarity and molality are nearly equal. Why?",
 ["Water has a density of about 1.00 g/mL, so 1 L of a dilute solution contains about 1 kg of water","Molarity and molality are always equal","The solute has no mass","Water's molar mass is 18.02 g/mol","Molality is defined per liter of water"],0,
 "Molarity divides by liters of solution; molality by kg of solvent. In a dilute aqueous solution almost all of a liter is water, and 1 L of water has a mass of about 1 kg, so the two denominators are nearly the same number. For concentrated solutions (like 1.50 M NaCl → 1.54 m, or 30% H₂O₂: 9.79 M vs 12.6 m) they diverge.",
 ["Compare the denominators: L of solution vs kg of water.","What is the mass of 1 L of water?","1 L ≈ 1 kg for dilute water solutions."]],
["m11-a-104",11,24,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Equal MASSES (10.0 g each) of the following are dissolved in separate 1.00 kg portions of water. Which solution has the LOWEST freezing point? (Assume ideal behavior.)",
 ["Glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆ (180.16 g/mol)","Sucrose, C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ (342.30 g/mol)","NaCl (58.44 g/mol)","CaCl₂ (110.98 g/mol)","Urea, CO(NH₂)₂ (60.06 g/mol)"],2,
 "With equal masses, count moles of PARTICLES: i × (10.0/M). Glucose 1 × 0.0555 = 0.0555; sucrose 1 × 0.0292 = 0.0292; NaCl 2 × 0.1711 = 0.342; CaCl₂ 3 × 0.0901 = 0.270; urea 1 × 0.1665 = 0.1665. NaCl gives the most particles, so it has the largest ΔT(f) and the lowest freezing point. CaCl₂ has the larger i, but its higher molar mass means fewer formula units per 10.0 g - the trap.",
 ["Equal masses are not equal moles.","Compute i × (10.0 g ÷ molar mass) for each.","NaCl: 0.342 mol of ions vs CaCl₂: 0.270 mol."]],
]);

/* ---------------- Gym 11 · Lesson 24: more typed-answer practice ---------------- */
chemAddQuestions([
["m11-a-105",11,24,3,"Calculation","num",
 "A 1.50 M aqueous NaCl solution has a density of 1.06 g/mL. What is the mass percent of NaCl? (Enter a number in %.)",
 ["8.27 %"],8.270,
 "Take 1.000 L of solution: mass = 1000 mL × 1.06 g/mL = 1060 g. NaCl present = 1.50 mol × 58.44 g/mol = 87.66 g. Mass percent = 87.66 ÷ 1060 × 100 = 8.27%. Dividing by 1000 g (as if the solution had the density of water) gives 8.77%.",
 ["Pick 1.000 L of solution as your sample.","Use the density for the solution's mass and the molarity for the NaCl mass.","87.66 g ÷ 1060 g × 100."]],
["m11-a-106",11,24,2,"Calculation","num",
 "What is the osmotic pressure of intravenous saline, 0.150 M NaCl, at body temperature (37 °C)? Assume i = 2; R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K). (Enter a number in atm.)",
 ["7.64 atm"],7.635,
 "Π = i·M·R·T = 2 × 0.150 × 0.08206 × 310.15 = 7.64 atm. This matches the osmotic pressure of blood, so red cells neither swell nor shrink in it (isotonic). Leaving out i gives 3.82 atm; using 37 instead of 310.15 K gives 0.911 atm.",
 ["Π = iMRT, T in kelvin.","i = 2 for NaCl; T = 310.15 K.","2 × 0.150 × 0.08206 × 310.15."]],
["m11-a-107",11,24,3,"Calculation","num",
 "A 0.0500 m aqueous NaCl solution freezes at −0.175 °C. K(f) = 1.86 °C/m. What is the measured van 't Hoff factor? (Enter a number.)",
 ["1.88"],1.882,
 "i = ΔT(f) ÷ (K(f)·m) = 0.175 ÷ (1.86 × 0.0500) = 0.175 ÷ 0.0930 = 1.88. It is a little below the ideal value of 2 because some Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions travel together as ion pairs; for singly charged ions in a dilute solution the effect is small.",
 ["Rearrange ΔT(f) = i·K(f)·m.","Use ΔT(f) = 0.175 °C (a positive size).","0.175 ÷ (1.86 × 0.0500)."]],
["m11-a-108",11,24,2,"Calculation","num",
 "By how many degrees Celsius is the boiling point of water raised in a 2.00 m aqueous sucrose solution? K(b) = 0.512 °C/m. (Enter a number in °C.)",
 ["1.02 °C"],1.024,
 "Sucrose is a nonelectrolyte, i = 1. ΔT(b) = i·K(b)·m = 1 × 0.512 × 2.00 = 1.02 °C, so the solution boils at 101.02 °C. Using K(f) (1.86) would give 3.72 °C.",
 ["Sucrose does not ionize.","ΔT(b) = K(b) × m.","0.512 × 2.00."]],
["m11-a-109",11,24,4,"Calculation","num",
 "A mixture is made from equal MASSES (50.0 g each) of ethanol (C₂H₅OH, 46.07 g/mol) and water. What is the mole fraction of ethanol? (Enter a number.)",
 ["0.281"],0.2812,
 "n(ethanol) = 50.0 ÷ 46.07 = 1.085 mol; n(water) = 50.0 ÷ 18.02 = 2.775 mol. X(ethanol) = 1.085 ÷ (1.085 + 2.775) = 0.281. Equal masses are not equal moles: answering 0.500 treats mass fraction as mole fraction, and 1.085/2.775 = 0.391 forgets to put ethanol into the total.",
 ["Mole fraction needs moles of each component.","Convert 50.0 g of each to moles with its own molar mass.","1.085 ÷ (1.085 + 2.775)."]],
]);
